Since the construction of our Gysin map relies heavily on the blowup excision of $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -spectra, we have to use various identities between nested blowups in order to prove Theorem 1.3. We are unable to find these identities in the literature at least in the derived case, so we include the basic theory of blowups in derived algebraic geometry as well as the proof of these identities in Appendix A.

#### Remark 1.4.

Of course, the Composition in Theorem 1.3 is handicapped from the $\infty$ -categorical viewpoint: a full composition compatibility ought to contain larger commutative diagrams for arbitrarily long chains of closed immersions. However, the full composition is already extremely complicated even in the $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant case as in [15]: it needs a significantly heavier theory of nested blowups than what we want to develop here in Appendix A, so we leave it to our future work.

### 1.2. A $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -unstable theory

Compared to the $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -homotopy theory, another missing aspect of the Annala–Hoyois–Iwasa $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -motivic homotopy theory is the unstable theory, i.e. a theory where the pointed $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ is not tensor-invertible. During the study of the Gysin map, we notice that we have only used blowup excision and $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -homotopy, but not $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -stability anywhere, so we propose a $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -unstable theory of non- $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant motives here:

#### Theorem 1.5 (Definition 2.6; Propositions 2.14, 2.15).

For any scheme $S$ , there is a pointed presentably symmetric monoidal $\infty$ -category $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ of $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spaces, which supports canonical presentably symmetric monoidal functors $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)\to\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ and $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)\to\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}_{S}$ , to the Morel–Voevodsky’s category of pointed $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -motivic spaces and the Annala–Hoyois–Iwasa’s category of unstable $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -motivic spectra, respectively.

Moreover, let $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(S)=\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)\otimes\mathsf{Sp}$ be the $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ -stabilization. Then the Gysin map in Theorem 1.1 and the isomorphisms in 1.3 all come from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(S)$ through the stabilized version $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(S)\to\mathsf{MS}_{S}$ of the above functor.

#### Remark 1.6.

In non- $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant motivic homotopy theory, the invertibility of $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ does not imply that of $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ , resulting in two stabilities as listed below:

| Category | $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ -stable | $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -stable |
|---|---|---|
| $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}$ | No | No |
| $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}$ | Yes | No |
| $\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}$ | No | Yes |
| $\mathsf{MS}$ | Yes | Yes |

In $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -motivic homotopy theory, $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -stability implies $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ -stability, and [27] managed to construct the Gysin map even before $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ -stabilization. However, in our non- $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant theory, we are only able to do so after $\mathrm{S}^{1}$ -stabilization.

### 1.3. Applications

Our construction yields Gysin maps for any cohomology theory satisfying mild conditions, and coincides with any existing construction:

#### Theorem 1.7 (Constructions 4.4, 4.6).

Let $\mathcal{D}\colon\mathsf{Sch}\to\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{st}}$ be a presheaf of stable presentably symmetric monoidal categories on derived schemes. Assume:

- (1) $\mathcal{D}$ is a Zariski sheaf;

- (2) $\mathcal{D}$ satisfies quasismooth blowup excision, in the sense that for every blowup ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Y}X}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces e}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces b}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces i}$ where $i$ is a quasismooth closed immersion, and for every $C\in\mathcal{D}(X)$ , we have $C=i_{*}i^{*}C\times_{e_{*}e^{*}C}b_{*}b^{*}C$ ;

- (3) for every $X$ smooth over $S$ and $C\in\mathcal{D}(X)$ , denote $C\langle-1\rangle=\cofib(C\to p_{*}p^{*}C)$ where $p\colon\mathbf{P}^{1}_{X}\to X$ is the projection; then $C\langle-1\rangle=C\otimes 1_{X}\langle-1\rangle$ , and the formation of $\langle-1\rangle$ commutes with pullbacks.

Then for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ of quasismooth derived $S$ -schemes and every $C\in\mathcal{D}(X)$ there is a natural Gysin map

$$ \gys_{C}(i)\colon i_{*}((i^{*}C)\langle-\mathcal{N}_{i}\rangle)\to C $$

where $\mathcal{N}_{i}$ is the conormal module, and for $B\in\mathcal{D}(Y)$ write $B\langle-\mathcal{N}_{i}\rangle=\fib(p_{*}p^{*}B\to q_{*}q^{*}B)$ where $p\colon\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to Y$ and $q\colon\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})\to Y$ are the projections.

In Constructions 4.4 and 4.6 in the main text, the setting will be more general and the conditions will be weaker. We will also provide a general comparison theorem, Theorem 4.9, which in particular implies, for étale cohomology, that the above theorem generalizes Gabber’s étale Gysin map [17], and for Hodge cohomology, that the above theorem generalizes the Gysin map in [32, 0G82] (Example 4.7).

Besides, during the preparation of this paper, various applications of Theorems 1.1 and 1.3 have already been out. See [2], [20], [5] and [1]. We list a few of their theorems here, which use our Gysin map as an essential input:

#### Theorem 1.8 ([2, Theorem 1.1]).

For any scheme $S$ and any projective smooth $S$ -scheme $X$ , $X$ is dualizable in $\mathsf{MS}_{S}$ with dual $\Th_{X}(-\Omega_{X/S})$ .

Not only does this theorem prove a Poincaré duality for virtually any cohomology theory for projective smooth schemes, it also has further arithmetic application [13] which really uses that the duality holds already in $\mathsf{MS}$ .

#### Theorem 1.9 ([2, Theorem 1.7], [29, Theorem C]).

Let $k$ be a perfect field. Then there is a canonical $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant cohomology theory $\mathrm{R}\Gamma_{\mathrm{crys},\mathbf{A}^{1}}\colon\mathsf{Sm}_{k}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathsf{D}(\Witt(k))$ such that whenever $U=X\setminus\partial X$ with $X$ projective smooth and $\partial X$ a strict normal crossing divisor, there is a canonical isomorphism

$$ \mathrm{R}\Gamma_{\mathrm{crys},\mathbf{A}^{1}}(U)=\mathrm{R}\Gamma_{\mathrm{crys},\log}(X,\partial X). $$

In short, log crystalline cohomology is independent of the good compactification.

In [2], this theorem is a clever application of the above dualizability which relies on our Gysin map. In [29], the proof is independent of this paper, using a version of the Gysin map in the log motivic setting constructed in that paper.

### 1.4. Notation and convention

We summarize our global notation and convention here, mainly regarding derived algebraic geometry. In short, everything is animated or derived unless otherwise stated, and readers familiar with the setup can safely skip this subsection. Precisely:

- (1) A category means an $(\infty,1)$ -category. For $n\in\mathbb{N}$ , an $n$ -category means an $(\infty,n)$ -category as in [6] for example. We will at most use $(\infty,2)$ -categories in this paper.

- (2) For $n\in\mathbb{N}$ , we use $[n]$ to denote the poset $(\{0,1,\ldots,n\},\leq)$ , often viewed as a category.

- (3) A ring is an animated ring.

- (4) For a ring $R$ , an $R$ -module is an animated $R$ -module, or in other words an object in $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(R)$ . The category $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(R)$ is left complete prestable and canonically presentably symmetric monoidal under $\otimes_{R}$ . We denote the presentable stabilization of $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(R)$ by $\mathsf{D}(R)$ and call its objects $R$ -complexes.

- (5) For a ring $R$ , an $R$ -algebra is a ring along with a map from $R$ to it. We let $\mathsf{Alg}(R)$ denote the category of $R$ -algebras, which is canonically presentably symmetric monoidal under $\otimes_{R}$ .

- (6) A surjection of rings, modules, or algebras means a map that is surjective on classical truncations, i.e. on $\pi_{0}$ .

- (7) A prestack is an accessible functor $\mathsf{Ring}\to\mathsf{Ani}$ . For a prestack $X$ and a ring $R$ , an $R$ -point of $X$ is a point in the anima $X(R)$ .

- (8) For an accessible topology $\tau$ on $\mathsf{Ring}$ , a $\tau$ -stack is a prestack satisfying $\tau$ -descent. A stack means a Zariski stack in this paper.

- (9) An affine scheme is a representable prestack. For a ring $R$ , we let $\Spec(R)$ denote the prestack represented by $R$ , i.e. the functor $\Map(R,-)$ , and we call it the spectrum of $R$ .

- (10) A scheme is a stack that is Zariski covered by Zariski open substacks that are affine schemes.

- (11) For a prestack $X$ , an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module is a natural transformation $X\to\mathsf{Mod}$ . $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -modules form a presentably symmetric monoidal left complete prestable category denoted $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(X)$ as each $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(R)$ is one. Similarly, we denote the presentable stabilization of $\mathsf{D}_{\geq 0}(X)$ by $\mathsf{D}(X)$ , and call its objects $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -complexes. Note that this notion is the derived analog of quasicoherent $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -modules rather than general $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -modules for any topos built from $X$ and the structure sheaf $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ thereon. We choose to omit the word “quasicoherent” because the latter notion does not make sense for a general prestack, and also because we only care about quasicoherent ones.

- (12) From the previous item, for a prestack map $Y\to X$ , an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module $\mathcal{M}$ naturally pulls back to an $\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ -module, which we denote by $\mathcal{M}|_{Y}$ .

- (13) For a prestack $X$ , an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebra is a natural transformation $X\to\mathsf{Alg}$ . $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebras form a presentably symmetric monoidal category as each $\mathsf{Alg}(R)$ is one. For an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebra $\mathcal{A}$ , its relative spectrum, denoted $\underline{\Spec}(\mathcal{A})$ , is the prestack over $X$ whose $R$ -point is the datum of an $x\in X(R)$ plus an $R$ -algebra map $\mathcal{A}(x)\to R$ . The structure map $\underline{\Spec}(\mathcal{A})\to X$ is clearly representable by affine schemes. Conversely, every prestack map representable by affine schemes is a relative spectrum.

- (14) A closed immersion of prestacks is a prestack map representable by ring surjections. Equivalently a closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ is the relative spectrum of an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebra $i_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ where the structure map $\mathcal{O}_{X}\to i_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ is a surjection. In this case, we call the $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module $\fib(\mathcal{O}_{X}\to i_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y})$ the ideal of the closed immersion $i$ , and we often just write $\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ to mean $i_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ . We will not try to define the notion of ideals seriously, but will rather view ideals only as modules with a map to $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ . In classical algebraic geometry, a closed immersion is a monomorphism, so one often calls it a closed substack. In contrast, in derived algebraic geometry, a general closed immersion is almost never a monomorphism, so we will avoid using the terminology closed substack.

- (15) A divisor is a closed immersion whose ideal is an invertible module. This generalizes the notion of an effective Cartier divisor in classical algebraic geometry to derived algebraic geometry.

- (16) For two closed immersions $Y\to X$ and $Z\to X$ , we let $Y+Z\to X$ denote the closed immersion given by the spectrum of the $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebra $\mathcal{O}_{Y}\times_{\mathcal{O}_{Y\times_{X}Z}}\mathcal{O}_{Z}$ , whose structure map is easily seen to be a surjection. More generally, for a finite family $(Y_{\omega}\to X)_{\omega\in\Omega}$ of closed immersions, denote by $\sum_{\omega\in\Omega}Y_{\omega}$ the closed immersion given by the $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebra $\lim_{\varnothing\neq\Psi\subseteq\Omega}\mathcal{O}_{Y_{\Psi}}$ , where $Y_{\Psi}$ is the fiber product of $(Y_{\omega})_{\omega\in\Psi}$ over $X$ (see Example A.19). This defines a commutative monoid structure on closed immersions to $X$ , where divisors form a commutative submonoid.

- (17) For a ring $A$ and an $A$ -module $M$ , we define $\mathbf{V}_{A}(M)=\Spec(\LSym^{\bullet}(M))$ and call it the total space of $M$ . By definition, its $R$ -point is the data of a ring map $A\to R$ and an $R$ -linear map $M\otimes_{A}R\to R$ . This construction globalizes to an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module $\mathcal{M}$ for a prestack $X$ , which we denote by $\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{M})$ or $\mathbf{V}(\mathcal{M})$ .

- (18) For a ring $A$ and an $A$ -module $M$ , we define $\mathbf{P}_{A}(M)=\Proj(\LSym^{\bullet}(M))$ and call it the projective space of $M$ . By definition, its $R$ -point is the data of a map $A\to R$ , an invertible $R$ -module $L$ , and a surjection $M\otimes_{A}R\to L$ . This is a scheme, and is proper over $\Spec(A)$ if $M$ is finitely generated. This construction globalizes to an $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module $\mathcal{M}$ for a prestack $X$ , which we denote by $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{M})$ or $\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{M})$ .

- (19) For a ring map $R\to A$ , the cotangent module $\mathrm{L}_{A/R}$ is the $A$ -module whose map to an $A$ -module $M$ amounts to a section of the trivial square-zero extension $A\oplus M\to A$ treated as an $R$ -algebra map. When $R\to A$ is surjective, $\pi_{0}\mathrm{L}_{A/R}=0$ , and we call $\mathrm{L}_{A/R}[-1]$ the conormal module and denote it by $\mathrm{N}_{A/R}$ . These constructions globalize at least to prestack maps representable by schemes, which we denote by $\mathcal{L}_{Y/X}$ and $\mathcal{N}_{Y/X}$ for such a map $Y\to X$ . This usage of the letter N might seem strange, but it aligns with the previous two items so that a $Y$ -point in $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{Y/X})$ is a normal vector field of $Y$ in $X$ .

- (20) For a ring $R$ , we say that an $R$ -complex $M$ is pseudocoherent if for every $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ , there exists a perfect complex $M_{n}$ and a map $M_{n}\to M$ whose cofiber is $n$ -connective. We say that an $R$ -algebra $A$ is pseudocoherent if there exists a $d\in\mathbb{N}$ and an $R$ -algebra map $R[x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}]\to A$ such that $A$ is pseudocoherent as an $R[x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}]$ -module. This notion globalizes at least to prestack maps representable by schemes. This notion is called almost of finite presentation in [24, Definition 7.2.4.26] and [25, Definition 4.2.0.1], but we choose to follow [32, 067G,067X] and call it pseudocoherence to avoid potential confusion with almost mathematics.

- (21) Following [33, Definition 2.18], we say that a ring map $R\to A$ is $d$ -smooth, if it is pseudocoherent and its cotangent complex $\mathrm{L}_{A/R}$ has tor-amplitude $[0,d]$ . Moreover, smooth means $0$ -smooth, and quasismooth means $1$ -smooth. This notion globalizes at least to prestack maps representable by schemes.

The above is not to introduce derived algebraic geometry but only to resolve possible ambiguity. For introductions, see [14, §5.1], [30, Part 1], and [25].

