## 2. $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -unstable motivic spectra

In this section, we introduce a minimal version of motivic spectra where the Gysin map is available. It will be very similar to but a little more general than the version in [4] and [3], and will clarify the structures that we actually use in constructing the Gysin map. Impatient readers who know the theory in [3] can skip this section and do everything with their motivic spectra, i.e. read all the $\mathcal{P}$ as $\mathsf{Sm}$ and read all the $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ as the Nisnevich version of $\mathsf{MS}_{S}$ .

All our schemes are quasicompact and quasiseparated.

We choose a regular cardinal $\kappa$ which can possibly be $\aleph_{0}$ , and denote by $\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa}$ the ( $\kappa$ -presentable, see [31, Theorem 2.9]) category of all $\kappa$ -presentable categories, as in [31, Definition 2.7].

### 2.1. Basic definitions

#### Definition 2.1 (motivic setup).

A $\mathrm{qbu}$ -motivic setup, or simply a motivic setup, is a wide subcategory $\mathcal{P}\subseteq\mathsf{Ring}^{\kappa}$ , i.e. a set $\mathcal{P}$ of maps of $\kappa$ -compact animated rings closed under composition, such that:

- (1) $\mathcal{P}$ is closed under base change;

- (2) $\mathcal{P}$ contains open immersions;

- (3) $\mathcal{P}$ is Zariski local on both the source and the target; accordingly, we can talk about a scheme map being in $\mathcal{P}$ , meaning Zariski locally so;

- (4) $\mathcal{P}$ contains projective bundles, i.e. for any scheme $Y$ and any vector bundle $\mathcal{E}/Y$ , the projective bundle map $\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})\to Y$ lies in $\mathcal{P}$ ;

- (5) $\mathcal{P}$ is closed under quasismooth blowups, i.e. for any scheme $S$ and any quasismooth closed immersion $Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , the blowup $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ (see [19, Definition 4.3], denoted as $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y/X}$ loc. cit.) lies in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ .

Note that in the situation of (5) the exceptional divisor $\mathrm{E}_{Y}X$ also lies in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , due to (4) and quasismoothness: by the discussion at the end of [19, §4.3], $\mathrm{E}_{Y}X=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{N}_{Y/X})$ is the projective bundle of a vector bundle.

For $R\in\mathsf{Ring}$ not $\kappa$ -compact, we define $\mathcal{P}_{R}\subseteq\mathsf{Ring}_{R}^{\kappa}$ by extending via $\kappa$ -filtered colimits from $\kappa$ -compact rings, and define $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ for general schemes $S$ by descent.

#### Example 2.2.

Examples of motivic setups include:

- (1) The class $\mathsf{Sm}$ of smooth morphisms. In this case, for any scheme $S$ , every map between smooth $S$ -schemes is quasismooth.

- (2) More generally, for any $d\in\mathbb{N}$ , the class $d\text{-}\mathsf{Sm}$ of $d$ -smooth morphisms as in §1.4(21); when $d=1$ this is the usual quasismoothness.

- (3) The class of pseudo-coherent morphisms as in §1.4(20), if $\kappa>\aleph_{0}$ .

- (4) The class of perfect morphisms as in [32, 0685], if $\kappa>\aleph_{0}$ . Note that the notion obviously generalizes to derived algebraic geometry.

In this paper, whenever we specialize, we only use (1) or occasionally (2).

From now on, we fix a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ .

#### Definition 2.3 (quasismooth blowup excision).

Let $S$ be a scheme. We say that a presheaf on $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ satisfies quasismooth blowup excision, if for any quasismooth closed immersion $Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , its blowup square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Y}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

is sent to a pullback square. We let $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ denote the category of Zariski sheaves of animas on $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ that satisfy quasismooth blowup excision. In other words, $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is the accessible localization of $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ along morphisms of the form $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X\sqcup_{\mathrm{E}_{Y}X}Y\to X$ .

Note that $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is compactly generated, with a symmetric monoidal structure preserving compact objects induced by the cartesian product of $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , as well as a symmetric monoidal functor $\mathcal{P}_{S}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ obtained from Yoneda. We often abuse notation to let $X\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ denote its image in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ or the pointed category $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ (thus omitting the $(-)_{+}$ notation), and for a map $Y\to X$ in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , to let $X/Y$ denote its cofiber in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ .

#### Remark 2.4.

The reason that we only impose quasismooth blowup excision is very practical: the non- $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant cohomology theories that we want to study in practice only satisfy quasismooth blowup excision; see [7, §9.4]. Of course, one can impose stronger excision conditions specified by larger classes of closed immersions, study the corresponding motivic theories, and construct the Gysin map as in Construction 3.1 below. However, even if your cohomology satisfies stronger excision conditions, the Thom spectrum appearing in the Gysin map still looks weird when the conormal module is not finite locally free; it seems that one should not think of it as a twist in any sense.

#### Remark 2.5.

In fact, our entire theory carries over for the base $S$ in Definition 2.3 being merely a stack (or even an analytic stack, as long as the blowup behaves the same way as it does here) rather than a scheme, with the category $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ taken as that of stacks over $S$ with structure maps representable by scheme maps in $\mathcal{P}$ . The reason that we only allow a scheme base is twofold:

- • The case of a scheme base already illustrates our ideas very well.

- • We are not sure that the categories $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ and $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ below still capture a good notion of motives over a stack $S$ .

Now we define our version of unstable $\mathbf{P}$ -motivic spaces and spectra.

#### Definition 2.6 ( $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic space).

For a scheme $S$ , consider the following three $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear functors from $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ to $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ :

- • $\mathrm{b}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ defined as $\mathrm{b}(X,\mathcal{L})=X$ ;

- • $\mathrm{v}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ defined as $\mathrm{v}(X,\mathcal{L})=\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ ;

- • $\mathrm{t}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ defined as $\mathrm{t}(X,\mathcal{L})=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})}$ ;

consider also the following three $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear natural transformations:

- • $0\colon\mathrm{b}\to\mathrm{v}$ given by the closed immersion of the zero section;

- • $\mathrm{j}\colon\mathrm{v}\to\mathrm{t}$ given by the natural open immersion $\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ ;

- • $\mathrm{z}\colon\mathrm{v}\to\mathrm{t}$ that takes everything to the zero section $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ ;

obviously $\mathrm{j}\circ 0=\mathrm{z}\circ 0$ .

Define $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ as the initial object in $\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}/}$ equipped with a homotopy $h\colon\mathrm{j}=\mathrm{z}$ and a homotopy between its composite with $0\colon\mathrm{b}\to\mathrm{v}$ and the obvious $\mathrm{j}\circ 0=\mathrm{z}\circ 0$ mentioned above, all as $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear natural transformations between functors from $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ to $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Call $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ the category of $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spaces over $S$ . Define $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})=\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathsf{Sp}$ and call it the category of $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spectra over $S$ .

We use the notation $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}$ just to emphasize that it is pointed, not to say that it has an unpointed variant. When $\mathcal{P}=\mathsf{Sm}$ is the wide subcategory of smooth maps, we denote $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathsf{Sm}_{S})$ by $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ and $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathsf{Sm}_{S})$ by $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(S)$ for brevity.

#### Remark 2.7 (intuition of $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}$ and $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}$ ).

Loosely speaking, $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is obtained from $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ by freely adding a homotopy between the maps $\mathrm{j},\mathrm{z}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})\rightrightarrows\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})}$ , functorially in $(X,\mathcal{L})\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ . In particular, in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , after composed with $\mathrm{j}$ , any section $s\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ is canonically homotopic to the zero section, and when $s=0$ this homotopy is canonically the identity.

In $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , stability gives the splitting $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})}\oplus\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ due to the projection map $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to X=\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ . Let $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}},\tilde{\mathrm{z}}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})\rightrightarrows\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ denote the open immersion and the projection composed with the zero section immersion, respectively. Then under the above splitting, on the component $\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})}$ they become $\mathrm{j}$ and $\mathrm{z}$ , and on the component $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ they both become the projection map $\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})\to X=\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ . Therefore, in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , there is a canonical homotopy $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}=\tilde{\mathrm{z}}$ which similarly becomes the obvious $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}\circ 0=\tilde{\mathrm{z}}\circ 0$ after composed with the zero section $0\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ , functorially in $(X,\mathcal{L})\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , and in particular, after composed with $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}$ , any section $s\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ is canonically homotopic to the zero section, and when $s=0$ this homotopy is canonically the identity.

In our Construction 3.1 of the Gysin map, we will only use this fact in the case $\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{O}$ and $s=1$ . However, we will need the full generality of this fact through Proposition 2.19 when proving Theorem 3.10, the normalization of the Gysin map (i.e. calculating the Gysin map of the zero section immersion into a vector bundle).

#### Remark 2.8 (well-definedness).

The existence of the initial object in Definition 2.6 is guaranteed by the adjoint functor theorem as follows:

Consider the functor $\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}/}\to\mathsf{Ani}$ that takes a $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ -algebra $\mathcal{A}$ to the anima of homotopies $h\colon\mathrm{j}=\mathrm{z}$ such that after composed with $0\colon\mathrm{b}\to\mathrm{v}$ it becomes the obvious $\mathrm{j}\circ 0=\mathrm{z}\circ 0$ , all as $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear natural transformations between functors from $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ to $\mathcal{A}$ . This functor is clearly $\kappa$ -accessible and preserves all limits, so it has a left adjoint, whose value at the singleton is the desired $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\in\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}/}$ .

For regular cardinals $\kappa<\lambda$ , using the adjunction $\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa}\rightleftarrows\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\lambda}$ where the left adjoint is the inclusion and is symmetric monoidal, we see that $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is insensitive to the regular cardinal $\kappa$ . In particular, taking $\kappa=\aleph_{0}$ , we see that $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is compactly generated, its symmetric monoidal structure preserves compact objects, and the defining symmetric monoidal functor $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ preserves compact objects. Clearly the same hold for $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ .

#### Remark 2.9 ( $f^{*}$ -functoriality).

Let $f\colon T\to S$ be a scheme map. Left Kan extending the base change functor from $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ to $\mathcal{P}_{T}$ gives a symmetric monoidal functor $f^{*}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})$ , hence $f^{*}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ and $f^{*}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})_{*}$ , and hence $f^{*}\colon\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{T})$ and $f^{*}\colon\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{T})$ , all symmetric monoidal, by functoriality of Definition 2.6.

#### Remark 2.10 ( $f_{\sharp}$ -functoriality).

Let $S$ be a scheme and $f\colon T\to S$ be in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Left Kan extending the $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ -linear adjunction between the forgetful functor $\mathcal{P}_{T}\to\mathcal{P}_{S}$ and the base change functor $\mathcal{P}_{S}\to\mathcal{P}_{T}$ , we obtain a $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear adjunction

$$ f_{\sharp}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})\rightleftarrows\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S}):\!f^{*}. $$

Since $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})_{*}=\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}\otimes_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})}\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})$ and $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}=\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}\otimes_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})}\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{T})$ , the above $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear adjunction base changes to a $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear adjunction

$$ f_{\sharp}\colon\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{T})\rightleftarrows\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S}):\!f^{*}, $$

and similarly a $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear adjunction

$$ f_{\sharp}\colon\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{T})\rightleftarrows\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S}):\!f^{*}. $$

#### Remark 2.11 (changing topology).

Let $\tau$ be a topology on $\mathsf{Ring}^{\kappa}$ finer than the Zariski topology such that every $\tau$ -cover can be refined by one with maps in $\mathcal{P}$ . Then we have an obvious variant of Definition 2.6 with $\mathsf{Sh}_{\tau,\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ in place of $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , which in fact results in the category $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})}\mathsf{Sh}_{\tau}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . For example, $\tau=\mathrm{Nis}$ might be more morally correct for motivic homotopy theory. However, the main results in this paper only use the Zariski descent.

#### Remark 2.12 (changing setup).

Let $\mathcal{P}^{\prime}$ be a motivic setup containing $\mathcal{P}$ . Left Kan extending the inclusion $\mathcal{P}_{S}\to\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{S}$ yields symmetric monoidal functors $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{S})$ and $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{S})$ by the same procedure as Remark 2.9.

#### Remark 2.13 (an alternative theory).

In Definition 2.6, instead of $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , it might actually be more natural to consider the symmetric monoidal category $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}\in\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})/}$ defined as follows:

- • an object is a pair $(X,\mathcal{E})$ with $X\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ and $\mathcal{E}$ a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module;

- • a map $(Y,\mathcal{F})\to(X,\mathcal{E})$ is a map $f\colon Y\to X$ in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ along with a surjection $f^{*}\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{F}$ of quasicoherent $\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ -modules, therefore inducing a closed immersion $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{F})\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\times_{X}Y$ of vector bundles over $Y$ ;

- • the symmetric monoidal structure is given by the external direct sum;

- • the structure map $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}$ is given by $X\mapsto(X,0)$ ;

then consider the following two maps in $\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})/}$ :

- • $\mathrm{v}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ defined as $\mathrm{v}(X,\mathcal{E})=\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ ;

- • $\mathrm{t}\colon\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ defined as $\mathrm{t}(X,\mathcal{E})=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}$ , which is symmetric monoidal by [3, §3];

then freely add a homotopy between the two $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear symmetric monoidal natural transformations $\mathrm{v}\rightrightarrows\mathrm{t}$ given by the natural open immersion and the zero section, and define $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}^{\prime}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\in\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}/}$ as the resulting category.

By construction, there is a map $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}^{\prime}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ in $\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\kappa})_{\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}/}$ , roughly because Definition 2.6 is just using the rank $\leq 1$ part of $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}$ without the symmetric monoidal structure.

One reason that we stick to the current Definition 2.6 is that, there is no symmetric monoidality statement in [3, Theorem 4.1] (although expected), which is crucial for comparing $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}^{\prime}_{*}$ with $\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}$ as in Proposition 2.15 below. In fact, other remarks and propositions in this subsection work well or even better with $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}^{\prime}_{*}$ .

### 2.2. Comparing with other motivic theories

The following propositions show that our $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spectrum is more general than both the pointed $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -motivic space in [27] and the $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -motivic spectrum in [3].

#### Proposition 2.14.

For a scheme $S$ , let $\mathsf{H}(S)$ denote the category of $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -motivic spaces, i.e. the category of $\mathbf{A}^{1}$ -invariant Nisnevich sheaves on $\mathsf{Sm}_{S}$ . Let $\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ denote the pointed category. Then there is a unique $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathsf{Sm}_{S})_{*}$ -linear symmetric monoidal functor from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ to $\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ , which is moreover functorial in $S$ .

#### Proof.

It suffices to check the following:

- • Sheaves in $\mathsf{H}(S)$ satisfy quasismooth blowup excision. By [3, Proposition 2.2], it suffices to check that the blowup square of $0\colon Y\to\mathbf{A}^{n}_{Y}$ is sent to a pullback square. This is easy, as in this case both $Y\to\mathbf{A}^{n}_{Y}$ and $\mathbf{P}^{n-1}_{Y}=\mathrm{E}_{Y}\mathbf{A}^{n}_{Y}\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}\mathbf{A}^{n}_{Y}=\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{P}^{n-1}_{Y}}(\mathcal{O}(1))$ are sent to isomorphisms.

- • There is a unique homotopy in $\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ between the two maps $\mathrm{j},\mathrm{z}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})\rightrightarrows\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{L})}$ that restricts to the identity homotopy on the zero section, functorially in $(X,\mathcal{L})\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathsf{Sm}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ . This is obvious, since the zero section $0\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{L})$ is an equivalence in $\mathsf{H}(S)$ . ∎

#### Proposition 2.15.

For a scheme $S$ , let $\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ denote the presentably symmetric monoidal category obtained from the pointed category $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ by smash-inverting $\mathbf{P}^{1}_{S}/\infty$ , and let $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})=\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathsf{Sp}$ . Then there is a canonical $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -linear symmetric monoidal functor from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ to $\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , and hence one from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ to $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , functorially in $S$ .

In particular, with the Nisnevich version of the theory in [3], we have a canonical functor from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(S)$ to $\mathsf{MS}^{\mathrm{un}}_{S}$ and hence one from $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(S)$ to $\mathsf{MS}_{S}$ .

#### Proof.

This is an immediate consequence of [3, Theorem 4.1]. ∎

#### Remark 2.16 (comparing with [11, Definition 3.6]).

Bouis and Kundu define in [11, Definition 3.6] a notion called deflatability for presheaves on schemes: for a scheme $S$ , they call a presheaf $F$ on $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ deflatable, if functorially in $T\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ there exists a homotopy between the maps

$$ F(\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T})\rightrightarrows F(\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}), $$

where the two maps are induced by $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}=\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}\setminus\infty\subset\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ and $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}\to T\xrightarrow{\infty}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ , respectively. At least a priori, this is a weaker condition (or structure) than our $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spectra. More precisely, if a presheaf $F$ factors through the canonical functor $\mathcal{P}_{S}\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega}$ , then $F$ is deflatable, because:

- • for such $F$ , Remark 2.7 implies that the map $F(\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T})\to F(\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T})$ induced by $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}=\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}\setminus\infty\subset\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ is canonically homotopic to that induced by $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}\to T\xrightarrow{0}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ , which is then canonically homotopic to that induced by $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}\to T\xrightarrow{\infty}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ again by Remark 2.7, because both $0$ and $\infty$ in $\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ factor through the inclusion $\mathbf{A}^{1}_{T}=\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}\setminus 1\subset\mathbf{P}^{1}_{T}$ .

The author thanks Toni Annala for pointing out [11, Definition 3.6] to him.

#### Remark 2.17.

During the preparation of this paper, Noam Zimhoni has proposed to the author another candidate for an unstable $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -motivic theory:

Let $\mathsf{Ani}^{\mathrm{hypo}}\subset\mathsf{Ani}$ denote the full subcategory of animas whose every component has a hypoabelian fundamental group, i.e. having no perfect subgroup. Then the inclusion has a left adjoint $(-)^{+}$ which is Quillen’s plus construction; see [28]. Now consider the initial presentable category $\mathbf{P}^{+}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ fitting into the diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Vect}_{S}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Vect}_{S}^{+}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{P}^{+}(\mathcal{P}_{S})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{P}(-)} $$

where $\mathrm{Vect}_{S}$ denotes the anima of vector bundles over $S$ and $\mathrm{Vect}_{S}^{+}$ its plus construction. This seems to be a plausible proposal because

$$ \begin{bmatrix}1&1\\
0&1\end{bmatrix}\in\pi_{1}(\mathrm{Vect}_{S},\mathcal{O}^{\oplus 2}) $$

is often killed in $\pi_{1}(\mathrm{Vect}_{S}^{+},\mathcal{O}^{\oplus 2})$ , thus giving a $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -homotopy between $0$ and $1$ in $\mathbf{P}^{+}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . If this is the case, our $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ should map to $\mathbf{P}^{+}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ .

### 2.3. Constructions in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}$

In this subsection, we develop constructions in $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spaces that will be used in our study of Gysin maps, starting with the Thom space.

#### Definition 2.18 (Thom space).

Let $S$ be a scheme, $X\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , and $\mathcal{E}$ be a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module. We define the Thom space $\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ as the quotient $\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}$ taken in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ , $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ or $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . When $X=S$ we often omit the subscript $X$ .

As mentioned in Remark 2.13, by [3, §3], this gives a symmetric monoidal functor $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}\ni(X,\mathcal{E})\mapsto\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ where the category $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathsf{Vect}^{\mathrm{emb}}}$ is defined in that remark, with only immersions of vector bundles as morphisms and the external direct sum as the symmetric monoidal structure.

The following is a version of [3, Proposition 4.9] and can be thought of as the Gysin map of the zero section immersion $X\xrightarrow{0}\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ . It will play a crucial role in the normalization of the Gysin map (Theorem 3.10).

#### Proposition 2.19.

Let $S$ be a scheme, $X\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , and $\mathcal{E}$ be a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module. Then the map

$$ \Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}\to\frac{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})}=\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0} $$

has a canonical retraction in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ functorially in $(S,X,\mathcal{E})$ , in the sense that:

- • For surjections $\mathcal{E}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{E}^{\prime}$ , the retraction is compatible with $\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E}^{\prime})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E}^{\prime})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E}^{\prime})\setminus 0}$ mapping to $\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{[1]}$ .

- • For maps $X^{\prime}\to X$ , the retraction is compatible with $\Th_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}$ mapping to $\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{[1]}$ .

- • For maps $g\colon S\to T$ in $\mathcal{P}$ , the retraction is compatible with the identification between $g_{\sharp}\left(\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}\right)$ and $\left(\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}\right)\in\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{T})^{[1]}$ .

- • For maps $f\colon S^{\prime}\to S$ , the retraction is compatible with the identification between $f^{*}\left(\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}\right)$ and $\left(\Th_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{X^{\prime}}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}\right)\in\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S^{\prime}})^{[1]}$ , where $X^{\prime}=X\times_{S}S^{\prime}$ .

#### Proof.

It suffices to factor the open immersion $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ through the closed immersion $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ canonically in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Since they both factor through $\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ , we may as well try to factor the open immersion $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ through the closed immersion $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ . Note that

$$ \mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}(\mathcal{O}(-1)),\,\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})}(\mathcal{O}(-1)\oplus\mathcal{O}) $$

compatible with the open immersion above, and the closed immersion above is the zero section. Now Remark 2.7 gives the desired factorization. ∎

#### Remark 2.20.

Since $\mathcal{E}\mapsto\frac{\mathbf{V}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}$ is obviously symmetric monoidal as a functor $(\mathsf{Vect}_{S}^{\mathrm{emb}},\oplus)\to\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ , Proposition 2.19 gives a symmetric monoidal structure of $\Th(-)\colon(\mathsf{Vect}_{S}^{\mathrm{emb}},\oplus)\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Since the blowup loci occurring in [3, §3] are away from the zero section, one can see that this symmetric monoidal structure coincides with the one from Definition 2.18 after composed with the symmetric monoidal functor $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\to\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , as follows:

For a finite set of objects $\mathcal{E}=(\mathcal{E}_{i})_{i\in I}$ in $\mathsf{Vect}_{S}^{\mathrm{emb}}$ , we have a commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\prod_{i\in I}\Th(\mathcal{E}_{i})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{E})/\partial\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{E})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th\left(\bigoplus_{i\in I}\mathcal{E}_{i}\right)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\prod_{i\in I}\frac{\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus 0}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\frac{\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\frac{\mathbf{P}\left(\bigoplus_{i\in I}\mathcal{E}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O}\right)}{\mathbf{P}\left(\bigoplus_{i\in I}\mathcal{E}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O}\right)\setminus 0}} $$

in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ by [3, Proposition 3.6], whose horizontal maps are all isomorphisms. The upper row is the symmetric monoidal structure of $\Th(-)$ while the lower one is that of $\frac{\mathbf{V}(-)}{\mathbf{V}(-)\setminus 0}$ . Now Proposition 2.19 gives retractions of both the left and the right vertical arrows in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . By the definition of retractions, this already identifies the two symmetric monoidal structures of $\Th(-)$ , without knowing that the retraction on the left coincides with the one on the right.

We can also reproduce [3, Theorem 4.1] in our unstable setting.

#### Proposition 2.21.

Let $S$ be a scheme, $X\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , and $\mathcal{E}$ be a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module. Then there is a canonical homotopy between $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ and $\tilde{\mathrm{z}}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to X=\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ functorially in $(S,X,\mathcal{E})$ . Let $\sigma\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ be a section and let $0\colon X\to\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ denote the zero section. Then there is a canonical homotopy between $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}\circ\sigma$ and $\tilde{\mathrm{\jj}}\circ 0$ functorially in $(S,X,\mathcal{E},\sigma)$ , which is the identity when $\sigma=0$ .

