compatible with the open immersion above. Therefore, Remark 2.7 gives a canonical factorization of $\mathrm{Bl}_{0}\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{0}\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ through $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})/}$ , which implies the claim by pushing out along $\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to X$ . ∎

#### Remark 2.22.

The exact same proof shows that there is a canonical homotopy between $\mathrm{j}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ and $\mathrm{z}\colon\mathbf{V}_{X}(\mathcal{E})\to X=\mathbf{P}_{X}(\mathcal{O})\to\Th_{X}(\mathcal{E})$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{H}_{*}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ functorially in $(S,X,\mathcal{E})$ . However, this does not make Remark 2.13 redundant, because this homotopy is not known to be symmetric monoidal in $(X,\mathcal{E})$ .

## 3. Construction and properties

In this section, we construct the Gysin map in our $\mathbf{P}$ -unstable motivic spectra and establish its basic properties. Throughout this section, we fix a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ and a base scheme $S$ . Again, impatient readers can read all the $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ here as the Nisnevich version of $\mathsf{MS}_{S}$ in [3].

