and take its cofiber in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ . We get a cofiber sequence

$$ \Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})\to\mathrm{Q}(i)\to X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0 $$

in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ , where $\mathrm{Q}(i)=\frac{\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})}{\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times 0)}$ and we are using $\frac{X\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}{X\times 0}=X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0$ . We call it the fundamental cofiber sequence of $i\colon Y\to X$ . The closed immersion $X=X\times 1\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})$ gives a map $X\to\mathrm{Q}(i)$ , and Remark 2.7 gives a nullhomotopy of its composition to $X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , so we get a map

$$ \gys(i)\colon X\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}) $$

in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , which we call the Gysin map, or the Pontryagin–Thom collapse map.

#### Remark 3.2.

Readers familiar with deformation to the normal cone will realize that $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{A}^{1})\setminus\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times 0)$ is the usual deformation to the normal cone of $i\colon Y\to X$ . Here we are using a compactified version of it.

Before looking at the functoriality of Gysin maps with respect to maps of closed immersions, we first recall the notion of excessive maps:

#### Definition 3.3 (excessive map, cf. [19, Definition 3.30]).

We say that a map $f$ of closed immersions

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f} $$

is excessive, if the map $f^{*}\mathcal{I}_{Y/X}\to\mathcal{I}_{Y^{\prime}/X^{\prime}}$ is surjective.

#### Remark 3.4 (comparing with [19, Definition 3.30]).

Keep the notation of Definition 3.3. [19, Definition 3.30] defines excessivity as:

- • after classical truncation, the above square becomes a pullback;

- • the map of conormal sheaves $f^{\prime*}\mathcal{N}_{Y/X}\to\mathcal{N}_{Y^{\prime}/X^{\prime}}$ is surjective.

In this remark, we prove that our Definition 3.3 coincides with theirs. For this, pulling back $Y$ along $f$ , one reduces to the case where $X^{\prime}=X$ . One also reduces to the affine case immediately. Therefore, it suffices to prove that for surjections of rings $A\to B\to B^{\prime}$ , the following are equivalent:

- (1) $\mathrm{I}_{B/A}\twoheadrightarrow\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/A}$ .

- (2) $\pi_{0}(B)=\pi_{0}(B^{\prime})$ and $\mathrm{N}_{B/A}\otimes_{B}B^{\prime}\twoheadrightarrow\mathrm{N}_{B^{\prime}/A}$ .

Now, using the fiber sequences

$$ \mathrm{I}_{B/A}\to\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/A}\to\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/B} $$

and

$$ \mathrm{N}_{B/A}\otimes_{B}B^{\prime}\to\mathrm{N}_{B^{\prime}/A}\to\mathrm{N}_{B^{\prime}/B} $$

one reduces to the case where $A=B$ . In this case we want to prove that the following are equivalent:

- (1) $\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/B}$ is $1$ -connective.

- (2) $\pi_{0}(B)=\pi_{0}(B^{\prime})$ and $\mathrm{N}_{B^{\prime}/B}$ is $1$ -connective.

Obviously, $1$ -connectivity of $\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/B}$ implies $\pi_{0}(B)=\pi_{0}(B^{\prime})$ , so we only need to prove that if $B=\pi_{0}(B)=\pi_{0}(B^{\prime})$ , then $1$ -connectivities of $\mathrm{I}_{B^{\prime}/B}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{B^{\prime}/B}$ are equivalent. This follows from Lemma 3.5 below.

#### Lemma 3.5.

Let $A\to B$ be a ring surjection where $\ker(\pi_{0}(A)\to\pi_{0}(B))$ is a nilpotent ideal in $\pi_{0}(A)$ . Then for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$ , the following are equivalent:

- (1) $\mathrm{I}_{B/A}$ is $n$ -connective.

- (2) $\mathrm{N}_{B/A}$ is $n$ -connective.

#### Proof.

Since $\ker(\pi_{0}(A)\to\pi_{0}(B))$ is nilpotent, one can base change the map along itself, and assume without loss of generality that it has a retraction $B\to A$ . Then $\mathrm{I}_{A/B}=\mathrm{I}_{B/A}[1]$ and $\mathrm{N}_{A/B}=\mathrm{N}_{B/A}[1]\otimes_{B}A$ , so the lemma is equivalent to the same claim for $B\to A$ with $n$ replaced by $n+1$ . This way one can reduce to the case where $\mathrm{I}_{B/A}$ is sufficiently connective, in particular $1$ -connective. Suppose $\mathrm{I}_{B/A}$ is $m$ -connective but not $(m+1)$ -connective for some $m\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ ; we want to prove the same for $\mathrm{N}_{B/A}$ . Now [25, Proposition 25.3.6.1] says that the universal derivation

$$ \cofib(A\to B)\otimes_{A}B\to\mathrm{L}_{B/A} $$

has an $(m+3)$ -connective fiber, which means that its $(-1)$ -shift

$$ \mathrm{I}_{B/A}\otimes_{A}B\to\mathrm{N}_{B/A} $$

has an $(m+2)$ -connective fiber, which clearly implies our claim. ∎

In fact, the Gysin map is functorial with respect to excessive maps:

#### Remark 3.6 (functoriality).

Keep the notation in Construction 3.1, and let

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

be an excessive map of closed immersions in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Then by functoriality of the blowup as in Definition A.6 or [19, §4.5], we see that Construction 3.1 is also functorial in the sense that there is a canonical commutative square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y^{\prime}}(\mathcal{N}_{i^{\prime}})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i^{\prime})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i)} $$

In particular, since $\mathcal{P}$ contains open immersions, we can take $X^{\prime}=X\setminus Y$ and $Y^{\prime}=\varnothing$ , and the above commutative square becomes

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X\setminus Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i)} $$

so the Gysin map automatically factors through $\frac{X}{X\setminus Y}$ . By abuse of notation, we often use $\gys(i)$ to also denote the resulting map $\frac{X}{X\setminus Y}\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ , and also call it the Gysin map.

#### Remark 3.7.

The rest of this section is devoted to some of the nontrivial properties of the Gysin map, including:

- (1) symmetric monoidality: for finitely many closed immersions, the Gysin map of their product is the tensor product of their Gysin maps;

- (2) normalization: the Gysin map of the zero section immersion of a vector bundle is the canonical quotient map;

- (3) composition: the Gysin map of a composition of closed immersions is the composition of the Gysin maps;

(1) will be addressed in Theorem 3.9, (2) will be addressed in Theorem 3.10, and the composition-of-two case of (3) will be addressed in Theorem 3.17. The composition-of- $n$ case of (3) (full coherence of composition) will be addressed in future work.

Our proofs of these properties extensively use blowups of more complicated diagrams of closed immersions, whose theory is developed in Appendix A for our need. We introduce the blowup of a poset of closed immersions in Definition A.6 for the proof of (1), and the pushout-blowup of an excessive square of closed immersions in Definition A.25 for the proof of the composition-of-two case of (3).

### 3.2. Symmetric monoidality

For symmetric monoidality, we introduce a version of Construction 3.1 for a finite family of quasismooth closed immersions, which we will call the multiple deformation space, as it is used to multiply Gysin maps. Beware that this is not the generalization of what people called the double deformation space before, which we will instead call the composite deformation space in Construction 3.15 below, as it is used to compose Gysin maps.

For any finite set $\Omega$ , any $\Omega$ -family $X=(X^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , and any $\Psi\subseteq\Omega$ , we denote $X^{\Psi}=\prod_{\omega\in\Psi}X^{\omega}$ and $X|_{\Psi}=(X^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Psi}$ for brevity, and similarly for maps.

#### Construction 3.8 (multiple deformation space).

For every finite set $\Omega$ and every $\Omega$ -family of quasismooth closed immersions $i=(i^{\omega}\colon Y^{\omega}\to X^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , we define the $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram of closed immersions

$$ \mathrm{C}(i)=(\mathrm{C}_{\Psi}(i)\colon Y^{\Psi}\times X^{\Omega\setminus\Psi}\times 0\to X^{\Omega}\times\mathbf{P}^{1})_{\Psi\subseteq\Omega} $$

in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , and consider its blowup $\mathrm{D}(i)=\mathrm{Bl}(\mathrm{C}(i))$ (Definition A.6). By Example A.19, $\mathrm{C}(i)$ is excessive (Definition A.10), so by Theorem A.20, $\mathrm{D}(i)$ and arbitrary intersections of the strict exceptional divisors $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i))_{\Psi\subseteq\Omega}$ remain in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Roughly speaking, Theorem A.20 exhibits $\mathrm{D}(i)$ as the iterated blowup of $X^{\Omega}\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ along

- • first $Y^{\Omega}$ ,

- • next the strict transform of $Y^{\Omega\setminus\omega}\times X^{\omega}$ for all $\omega\in\Omega$ ,

- • then that of $Y^{\Omega\setminus\Psi}\times X^{\Psi}$ for all $2$ -element subsets $\Psi\subseteq\Omega$ ,

- • …

- • and finally that of $X^{\Omega}$ ,

successively, and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i)$ as the strict transform of the exceptional divisor appearing in the step that blows up $Y^{\Psi}\times X^{\Omega\setminus\Psi}$ .

Similarly to Construction 3.1, form the quotient in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ of $\mathrm{D}(i)$ by the divisors $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i))_{\Psi\subsetneq\Omega}$ , which we denote by $\mathrm{Q}(i)$ , as well as that of $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{D}(i)$ by the divisors $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{D}(i)\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i))_{\Psi\subsetneq\Omega}$ , which we denote by $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)$ . More precisely, form the $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)$ -cube given by intersecting the divisors $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i))_{\Psi\in\mathrm{P}(\Omega)}$ , take total cofibers in all the directions except that of $\Omega\in\mathrm{P}(\Omega)$ (so $2^{|\Omega|}-1$ directions), and get a map $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)\to\mathrm{Q}(i)$ . By Theorem A.22, its cofiber is $X^{\Omega}\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ . In other words, in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ we have a cofiber sequence

$$ \mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)\to\mathrm{Q}(i)\to X^{\Omega}\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0 $$

which we call the fundamental cofiber sequence of $i=(i^{\omega}\colon Y^{\omega}\to X^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}$ . Note that $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{D}(i)$ is exactly the $\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ in [3, §3], and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)$ is the $\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{N}_{i})/\partial\mathbf{B}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ there. Similarly to Construction 3.1, the immersion $X^{\Omega}=X^{\Omega}\times 1\to X^{\Omega}\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ as well as its nullhomotopy in $X^{\Omega}\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0$ induces the multiple Gysin map

$$ \gys(i)\colon X^{\Omega}\to\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i). $$

When $\Omega$ is a singleton and there is only one immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ , we have $\mathrm{D}(i)=\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})$ , $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{D}(i)=\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\varnothing}\mathrm{D}(i)=\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ , so the $\mathrm{Q}(i)$ here is the $\mathrm{Q}(i)$ in Construction 3.1, the $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)$ here is the $\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ there, and the fundamental cofiber sequence here coincides with the one there.

Now, properties of such iterated blowups give rise to functorialities of the multiple deformation space with respect to the finite set $\Omega$ :

- (1) By Corollary A.23, the canonical map $\mathrm{D}(i)\to\mathrm{D}(i^{\Omega})$ induces an isomorphism from the fundamental cofiber sequence of $i$ to that of $i^{\Omega}\colon Y^{\Omega}\to X^{\Omega}$ , whose restriction $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Omega}\mathrm{Q}(i)\to\Th(\mathcal{N}_{i^{\Omega}})$ gives the one half of the symmetric monoidality construction as in [3, Proposition 3.6]. More generally, for any map $\varphi\colon\Omega\to\Gamma$ of finite sets, define the $\Gamma$ -family $\varphi_{*}i$ as

$$ (\varphi_{*}i)^{\gamma}=i^{\varphi^{-1}(\gamma)}\colon Y^{\varphi^{-1}(\gamma)}\to X^{\varphi^{-1}(\gamma)}; $$

then the canonical map $\mathrm{D}(i)\to\mathrm{D}(\varphi_{*}i)$ induces an isomorphism from the fundamental cofiber sequence of $i$ to that of $\varphi_{*}i$ , compatibly with composition of the map $\varphi$ .

- (2) For a map $\pi\colon\Omega\to\Xi$ of finite sets, thought of as a partition of $\Omega$ indexed by $\Xi$ , by Remark A.8(3), there is a canonical map $\mathrm{D}(i)\to\prod_{\xi\in\Xi}\mathrm{D}(i|_{\Omega_{\xi}})$ which takes $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\mathrm{D}(i)$ to $\prod_{\xi\in\Xi}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi_{\xi}}\mathrm{D}(i|_{\Omega_{\xi}})$ for $\Psi\subseteq\Omega$ , where $\Omega_{\xi}$ denotes the preimage $\pi^{-1}(\xi)$ of $\xi$ in $\Omega$ and similarly for $\Psi_{\xi}$ . For $\Psi=\Omega$ this map gives the other half of the symmetric monoidality construction as in [3, Proposition 3.6], which accordingly identifies $\gys(i)$ with $\bigotimes_{\xi\in\Xi}\gys(i|_{\Omega_{\xi}})$ , compatibly with refinements of partitions.

The following theorem is the analog of [3, §3] for Gysin maps.

#### Theorem 3.9 (symmetric monoidality).

Let $S$ be a scheme. The Gysin map is canonically symmetric monoidal as a functor taking quasismooth closed immersions in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ to arrows in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . In other words, for a finite family of quasismooth closed immersions $(i^{\omega}\colon Y^{\omega}\to X^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , there is a canonical commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigotimes_{\omega\in\Omega}X^{\omega}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigotimes_{\omega\in\Omega}\Th_{Y^{\omega}}(\mathcal{N}_{i^{\omega}})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\Omega}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y^{\Omega}}(\mathcal{N}_{i^{\Omega}})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigotimes_{\omega\in\Omega}\gys(i^{\omega})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i^{\Omega})} $$

in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , functorially with respect to $\Omega$ .

#### Proof.

This follows immediately from Construction 3.8. More precisely, a symmetric monoidal functor is a functor

$$ \mathsf{Span}(\mathsf{Fin})\times[1]\to\mathsf{Cat}. $$

In order to define a symmetric monoidal functor as promised, we have to specify categories and functors for objects and maps in $\mathsf{Span}(\mathsf{Fin})\times[1]$ compatibly with composition. Among these, the only data yet to be provided is for every span

$$ \Omega^{\prime}\leftarrow\Omega\to\Xi $$

a functor taking $\Omega^{\prime}$ -families of quasismooth closed immersions in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ to $\Xi$ -families of arrows in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Clearly, the only reasonable choice for this is

- • first pulling an $\Omega^{\prime}$ -family back to an $\Omega$ -family, and

- • next for each fiber $\Omega_{\xi}$ of the map $\Omega\to\Xi$ , using the multiple Gysin map in Construction 3.8 for the $\Omega_{\xi}$ -subfamily.

Then (1) and (2) of Construction 3.8 exactly guarantee the composition compatibility of this construction, thus defining the desired symmetric monoidal functor. ∎

### 3.3. Normalization

In fact, the normalization theorem by the author has appeared as [20, Proposition 4.7]. We include it here to emphasize that it holds in the generality of this paper.

#### Theorem 3.10 (normalization).

Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $Y\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ and $\mathcal{E}$ be a vector bundle on $Y$ . Let $0\colon Y\to\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ be the zero section. Then the Gysin map

$$ \gys(0)\colon\frac{\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})}{\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0}\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E}) $$

can be canonically identified with the retraction in Proposition 2.19 put in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . In particular, the Gysin map $\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})/\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})=\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ of the immersion $\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ is the canonical quotient map.

#### Proof.

Specializing the fundamental cofiber sequence in this case, we get

$$ \frac{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})}\to\frac{\mathrm{Bl}_{(0,0)}(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times\mathbf{P}^{1})}{\mathrm{Bl}_{(0,0)}(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times 0)}\to\frac{\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}{\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times 0} $$

and note that it splits in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ canonically by the map

$$ \mathrm{Bl}_{(0,0)}(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O}) $$

$$ (v,t)\mapsto[v:t] $$

which maps $\mathrm{Bl}_{(0,0)}(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\times 0)$ to $\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ . This canonically identifies the Gysin map $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ with the inclusion $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ (which is $v\mapsto[v:1]$ in coordinates) composed with the quotient map. Now it remains to identify the nullhomotopy of $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0\to\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ given by the Gysin map with the one in Proposition 2.19. By the above splitting, the former is given by

$$ (\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0)\times\mathbf{P}^{1}\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O}) $$

$$ (v,t)\mapsto[v:t] $$

as a $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -homotopy from the inclusion $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ to the composition $\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ ; since this $\mathbf{P}^{1}$ -homotopy canonically factors through $\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ with a commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0)\times\mathbf{A}^{1}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\setminus\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})}(\mathcal{O}(-1))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathbf{V}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\setminus 0)\times\mathbf{P}^{1}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})}\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})=\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})}(\mathcal{O}(-1)\oplus\mathcal{O})} $$

compatible with the proof of Proposition 2.19, we see that the two nullhomotopies are canonically equal. ∎

#### Corollary 3.11.

Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $Z\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ and $\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F}$ be vector bundles on $Z$ . Then the Gysin map of the immersion $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})$ can be expressed as

$$ \displaystyle\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}) \displaystyle\to\frac{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}=\frac{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}{\mathrm{E}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)\oplus\mathcal{O})}{\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1))} \displaystyle=\Th_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)), $$

where we note that $\mathcal{N}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})/\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}=\mathcal{E}(-1)$ .

In particular, taking quotient of the Gysin map of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ by that of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})$ , the resulting map

$$ \gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}))\colon\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)) $$

is an isomorphism.

#### Proof.

Since $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})$ and $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})$ are disjoint in $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})$ , the Gysin map in question factors through the quotient $\frac{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}=\frac{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}{\mathrm{E}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}$ and equals the Gysin map of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})$ factoring through the same quotient. Now the corollary follows from Theorem 3.10 by the identification $\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})=\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)\oplus\mathcal{O})$ that respects the $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})$ immersed in both sides. ∎

#### Corollary 3.12.

Let $S$ be a scheme and let $Z\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Let

$$ 0\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{F}\to 0 $$

be a short exact sequence of finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{Z}$ -modules. Note that in this situation we still have $\mathcal{N}_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})/\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{G})}=\mathcal{E}(-1)$ . Going through the equalities in Corollary 3.11 the other way, we get a map

$$ \gys(\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{G}))\colon\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{G})\to\Th_{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1))=\frac{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})}{\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{E})}. $$

Doing the above construction also for $\mathcal{G}\oplus\mathcal{O}\to\mathcal{F}\oplus\mathcal{O}$ and taking quotient, we get

$$ \gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G}))\colon\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G})\to\Th_{\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1))=\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}). $$

This map is an isomorphism in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ .

#### Proof.

One can assume $Z=S$ by $f_{\sharp}$ -functoriality. Isomorphy in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ can be checked Zariski locally on $S$ , which means that we can assume $\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}$ , in which case it follows from Corollary 3.11. ∎

#### Remark 3.13.

Note that the map $\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G}))$ in Corollary 3.12 and the map $\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}))$ in Corollary 3.11 can be made symmetric monoidal with respect to the direct sum, because the construction of $(\mathbf{B},\partial\mathbf{B})$ in [3, §3] is functorial and cartesian in surjections of locally free $\mathcal{O}_{Z}$ -modules.

The following proposition compares the Thom splitting isomorphism provided by Corollary 3.12 with the one coming from invertibility of Thom objects in $\mathsf{MS}$ .

#### Proposition 3.14 (comparing Thom splittings).

In $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , the isomorphism of Corollary 3.12 coincides with the one in the beginning of [3, §7].

#### Proof.

In fact, the latter isomorphism comes from the fact that the $\mathrm{K}$ -theory, as a Zariski sheaf, can be defined in two ways:

- (1) Quillen $+$ -construction, i.e. $\mathrm{K}=(\mathrm{Vect},\oplus)^{\mathrm{gp}}$ as the group completion of the $\mathbb{E}_{\infty}$ -monoid of vector bundles with respect to the direct sum;

- (2) Waldhausen $\mathrm{S}$ -construction, i.e. $\mathrm{K}=\Omega|\mathrm{S}_{\bullet}\mathsf{Vect}|$ as the loop space of the geometric realization of the simplicial anima $\mathrm{S}_{\bullet}\mathsf{Vect}$ defined as in [23, Lecture 18, Definition 9]. In short, a point in $\mathrm{S}_{n}\mathsf{Vect}$ is a vector bundle along with a length- $n$ filtration whose graded pieces are still vector bundles;

by (1), since $\Th_{Z}\colon(\mathrm{Vect}_{Z},\oplus)\to(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z}),\otimes_{Z})$ lands in invertible objects, it factors through $\mathrm{K}$ ; then by (2), the $2$ -cell

$$ (0\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{F}\to 0)\in\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect}_{Z} $$

witnesses an equality $[\mathcal{E}]+[\mathcal{F}]=[\mathcal{G}]$ in $\mathrm{K}=\Omega|\mathrm{S}_{\bullet}\mathsf{Vect}_{Z}|$ , so we get an isomorphism $\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E})\otimes\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})=\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G})$ accordingly. Composing it with the isomorphism of Corollary 3.12 gives a functor

$$ \mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect}_{Z}\to\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z})^{\mathrm{S}^{1}}:=\{(X,f)\mid X\in\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z}),\,f\colon X\xrightarrow{\sim}X\} $$

$$ \displaystyle(0\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{F}\to 0) \displaystyle\mapsto \displaystyle(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G}) \displaystyle\xrightarrow{\text{Corollary \ref{thom-splitting}}}\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})\xrightarrow{\mathrm{K}\text{-theory}}\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G})) $$

which is symmetric monoidal under the direct sum on the left and the pointwise tensor product on the right by Remark 3.13. Since Thom spaces are invertible in $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z})$ , the functor factors through the group completion of $\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect}_{Z}$ . Now, from the proof of the equivalence of the two ways to define the $\mathrm{K}$ -theory (more precisely, [23, Lecture 18, Theorem 10]), we see that the map

$$ (\mathrm{Vect}^{2},\oplus)\to(\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect},\oplus) $$

$$ (\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})\mapsto(0\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{F}\to 0) $$

of Zariski sheaves of $\mathbb{E}_{\infty}$ -monoids becomes an isomorphism after group completion. Therefore, to prove that the above functor $\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect}_{Z}\to\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z})^{\mathrm{S}^{1}}$ factors through $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z})\to\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{Z})^{\mathrm{S}^{1}}$ , $X\mapsto(X,\id_{X})$ , it suffices to do so after precomposed with the map $\mathrm{Vect}^{2}\to\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathsf{Vect}$ above, but this is clear because the isomorphism in Corollary 3.12 is defined as the composite of

$$ \gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G}))\colon\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{G})\to\Th_{\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)) $$

and the inverse of

$$ \gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}))\colon\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F})\to\Th_{\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{F})}(\mathcal{E}(-1)) $$

which is manifestly the identity for $\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{F}$ . ∎

