where the four arrows are divisors. When $Z\to Y\to X$ is understood, we omit it from the notation. By Definition A.25, these four blowups come with three strict exceptional divisors each. We denote $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{D}=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}\times 0}\mathrm{D}$ , $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{D}=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z\times 0\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}\mathrm{D}$ , and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z\times 0\times 0}\mathrm{D}$ , and similarly for $\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}$ , $\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}$ , and $\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}$ . By Theorem A.31, it is easy to see that $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}=\varnothing$ .

Now let $S$ be a scheme and suppose that $Z\to Y\to X$ is in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Define objects $\mathrm{Q},\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q},\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q},\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q}$ as the total cofibers of

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} $$

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} $$

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} $$

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Z}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\partial_{Y}\mathrm{D}}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\dfrac{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{D}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y,Z}\mathrm{D}}} $$

respectively, taken in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ , where $\cap$ for divisors means fiber products. Then by Theorem A.31, there is a canonical commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th(\mathcal{N}_{Z/X})\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Q}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Q}(Z\to X)\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th(\mathcal{N}_{Y/X})\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Q}(Y\to X)\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0} $$

in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}$ , whose rows and columns can be described as follow:

- (1) Its middle row is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ \mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1}) $$

quotiented by that of

$$ \mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(Y\times 0)\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(X\times 0). $$

- (2) Its upper row is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ \mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1}) $$

quotiented by that of

$$ \mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(Y\times 0)\to\mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(X\times 0). $$

- (3) Its lower row is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}\to X\times\mathbf{P}^{1} $$

quotiented by that of

$$ Y\times 0\to X\times 0. $$

- (4) Its middle column is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ Z\times\mathbf{P}^{1}=\mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(Z\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1}) $$

quotiented by that of

$$ \varnothing=\mathrm{Bl}_{Z\times 0}(Z\times 0)\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times 0). $$

- (5) Its left column is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ Z\times 0=\mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(Z\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathrm{E}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1}) $$

quotiented by that of

$$ \varnothing=\mathrm{E}_{Z\times 0}(Z\times 0)\to\mathrm{E}_{Y\times 0}(X\times 0). $$

- (6) Its right column is the fundamental cofiber sequence of

$$ Z\times\mathbf{P}^{1}\to X\times\mathbf{P}^{1} $$

quotiented by that of

$$ Z\times 0\to X\times 0. $$

Note that (2) and (5) give rise to isomorphisms

$$ \mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q}=\Th_{\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Z/Y})}((\mathcal{N}_{Y/X}|_{Z})(-1)) $$

and

$$ \mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\mathrm{Q}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\mathrm{Q}=\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Y/X}|_{Z}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{Z/Y}) $$

respectively, which compose to the equality at the end of Corollary 3.12.

The following lemma is an immediate consequence of the above construction.

#### Lemma 3.16.

Let $S$ be a scheme and let $Z\xrightarrow{j}Y\xrightarrow{i}X$ be a chain of two quasismooth closed immersions in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Then there is a canonical commutation of the square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{j})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(ij)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Z/i}))} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(0\colon Z\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} $$

in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , where

- (1) $\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Z/i}))$ means the Gysin map of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ quotiented by that of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij})$ as in Corollary 3.12 applied to

$$ 0\to j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\to\mathcal{N}_{ij}\to\mathcal{N}_{j}\to 0 $$

and hence is an isomorphism;

- (2) $\gys(0\colon Z\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ means the Gysin map of the composition $Z\to Y=\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ quotiented by that of $\varnothing\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ .

#### Proof.

This follows immediately from Construction 3.15 using the same method as in Construction 3.1, noting that the descriptions (1) and (2) above align exactly with (2) and (5) in Construction 3.15, respectively. ∎

#### Theorem 3.17 (composition).

Let $S$ be a scheme and let $Z\xrightarrow{j}Y\xrightarrow{i}X$ be a chain of two quasismooth closed immersions in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . Then there is a canonical commutation of the square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{j})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\otimes_{Z}\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(ij)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(i)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Z/i}))} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(\Th_{j}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} $$

in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ , where

- (1) $\gys(\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{Z/i}))$ means the Gysin map of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ quotiented by that of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{ij})$ as in Corollary 3.12 applied to

$$ 0\to j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\to\mathcal{N}_{ij}\to\mathcal{N}_{j}\to 0 $$

and hence is an isomorphism;

- (2) $\gys(\Th_{j}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ means the Gysin map of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ quotiented by that of $\mathbf{P}_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ .

#### Proof.

Lemma 3.16 reduces the question to identifying $\gys(0\colon Z\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ and $\gys(\Th_{j}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ , up to the symmetric monoidality of Definition 2.18. For this, apply Lemma 3.16 again, now on $Z\xrightarrow{0}\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ . Namely, apply Lemma 3.16 on $Z=\mathbf{P}_{Z}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ and $\varnothing\to\mathbf{P}_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ , and take quotient. This gives us the commutative square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{j})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\otimes_{Z}\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j})} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{j})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(0\colon Z\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\gys(\Th_{j}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} $$

where

- (1) the right vertical isomorphism is $\gys(\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\to\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i}\oplus\mathcal{N}_{j}))$ which by Corollary 3.11 is the identity;

- (2) the lower horizontal arrow is $\gys(0\colon Z\to\Th_{Z}(j^{*}\mathcal{N}_{i})\otimes_{Z}\Th_{Z}(\mathcal{N}_{j}))$ which by Theorem 3.10 and Remark 2.20 is the symmetric monoidal structure in Definition 2.18.

This finishes the proof. ∎

## 4. Comparisons and applications

In this section, we compare our Gysin map with existing constructions in various special cases, and give some applications of our Gysin map. As in the previous section, we fix a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ and a base scheme $S$ . For $X\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ denote its structure map by $a_{X}\colon X\to S$ .

### 4.1. Gysin maps for various cohomology theories

In this subsection, we use results in [4] to represent various cohomology theories as objects in $\mathsf{MS}$ , and thus construct Gysin maps for them.

#### Construction 4.1 (representing cohomology theories as motivic spectra).

Recall that $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is defined by smash-inverting the compact object $\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty$ in the compactly generated category $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S};\mathsf{Sp})$ with compact objects closed under smash products. Therefore, $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ is also compactly generated with compact objects closed under tensor products, and the full subcategory $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega}$ of compact objects is also given by smash-inverting $\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S};\mathsf{Sp})^{\omega}$ , in the world of small idempotent-complete stably symmetric monoidal categories.

Let $\mathrm{Fin}$ denote the anima of finite sets and $\bullet$ denote the singleton. View $\mathrm{Fin}$ as a symmetric monoidal category under the disjoint union, so it is the free symmetric monoidal category in the object $\bullet$ , and thus $\mathsf{P}(\mathrm{Fin};\mathsf{Sp})$ is the free stable presentably symmetric monoidal category in the object $\bullet$ . Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a $\mathsf{P}(\mathrm{Fin};\mathsf{Sp})[\bullet^{-1}]$ -module, and denote by $\langle-\Phi\rangle$ the action of the finite set $\Phi$ . In other words, $\mathcal{C}$ is a stable presentable category equipped with an automorphism $\langle-1\rangle$ commuting with itself.

Let $F\colon\mathcal{P}_{S}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ be a functor that we think of as a cohomology theory. By abuse of notation, denote also by $F$ the functor $\mathsf{P}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ given by right Kan extension and the functor $\mathsf{P}(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{*}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ given by the reduced cohomology. Assume:

- (1) $F$ is a Zariski sheaf;

- (2) $F$ satisfies quasismooth blowup excision;

- (3) there are identifications

$$ F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{\Phi}X)=F(X)\langle-\Phi\rangle $$

functorially for $X\in\mathcal{P}_{S}^{\mathrm{op}}$ and the finite set $\Phi$ in automorphisms and disjoint unions of finite sets, where $\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{\Phi}$ means smashing with $\Phi$ -many $\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty$ .

A notable special case of the above situation is when $\mathcal{C}$ is presentably symmetric monoidal, $F$ is lax symmetric monoidal, $F(S)=1_{\mathcal{C}}$ , $F(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)$ is invertible, and $F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}X)=F(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)\otimes F(X)$ . In this case $\langle-1\rangle$ is just tensoring with $F(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)$ .

Then I claim that canonically $F$ extends to an exact functor $(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ and in the special case above this extension is lax symmetric monoidal. In other words, $F$ can be represented by an object $F\in\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{C}$ , and in the special case above by an object $F\in\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{C})$ . Moreover, $F^{(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)^{\wedge\Phi}}=F\langle-\Phi\rangle$ functorially in $\Phi\in\mathrm{Fin}$ , identifying the $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ -twist of $F$ with the $\mathcal{C}$ -twist of $F$ .

Indeed, (1) and (2) extend $F$ to an exact functor $(\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S};\mathsf{Sp})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ ; then (3) gives $F$ a $\mathsf{P}(\mathrm{Fin};\mathsf{Sp})^{\omega}$ -linear structure (in the symmetric monoidal category of idempotent-complete stable categories), and thus extends $F$ further to a functor $(\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S};\mathsf{Sp})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}[(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)^{-1}]=(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{C}$ by extension of scalars, since $\bullet\in\mathrm{Fin}$ acts on $\mathcal{C}$ invertibly. Lax symmetric monoidality in the special case then follows easily, for example by the fact that extension of scalars is a symmetric monoidal $2$ -functor. The final claim follows from (3) immediately.

The author thanks Marc Hoyois for teaching him the above argument and even the current formulation of the above construction.

#### Remark 4.2 (orienting cohomology theories).

Keep the notation and the assumptions of Construction 4.1. By [4, Lemma 3.1.4], an orientation of $F$ as in [4, Definition 3.1.2] is a section of the $\mathrm{Fin}$ -linear natural transformation

$$ F(\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}\times-)\to F(\mathbf{P}^{1}\times-) $$

induced by $\mathcal{O}(1)\colon\mathbf{P}^{1}\to\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}$ . In the special case mentioned in Construction 4.1 where $\mathcal{C}$ is presentably symmetric monoidal and $F$ is lax symmetric monoidal, again by [4, Lemma 3.1.4], a section of the map

$$ F(\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}})\xrightarrow{F(\mathcal{O}(1))}F(\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\tilde{F}(\mathbf{P}^{1}):=\cofib(F(S)\to F(\mathbf{P}^{1})) $$

suffices, and in fact gives a linear orientation as in [4, Remark 3.1.5].

It is sometimes convenient to note that, since $\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}=\colim_{r}\mathbf{P}^{r}$ in $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ by [3, Theorem 5.3], an orientation of $F$ is equivalently a compatible family of sections of the $\mathrm{Fin}$ -linear natural transformations

$$ (F(\mathbf{P}^{r}\times-)\to F(\mathbf{P}^{1}\times-))_{r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}}, $$

and in the above special case a compatible family of sections of the maps

$$ (F(\mathbf{P}^{r})\to F(\mathbf{P}^{1}))_{r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}}. $$

#### Example 4.3 (localizing invariants).

Take our motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ to be all maps. Let $A$ be an accessible localizing invariant on $\mathsf{Perf}(S)$ -linear categories valued in a presentable stable category $\mathcal{C}$ . By [21, Theorems A and B] and the fact that localizing invariants satisfy Nisnevich descent, Construction 4.1 applies to $A(\mathsf{Perf}(-))$ with $\mathrm{Fin}$ acting on $\mathcal{C}$ trivially, and hence gives an object $A_{\mathsf{MS}}\in\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{C}$ with $A_{\mathsf{MS}}^{\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty}=A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ (cf. [4, §5.2]). [21, Theorem B] also gives compatible identifications $A_{\mathsf{MS}}^{\mathbf{P}^{r}}=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{r}A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ and thus gives $A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ an orientation by Remark 4.2.

This gives a Gysin map $A(\mathsf{Perf}(Y))\to A(\mathsf{Perf}(S))$ for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to S$ , and Theorem 4.9 below will show that it coincides with the obvious map induced by $i_{*}\colon\mathsf{Perf}(Y)\to\mathsf{Perf}(S)$ .

In practice we will mostly use Construction 4.1 in the following form:

#### Construction 4.4 (representing cohomology theories from $4$ -functor formalisms).

Let $\mathcal{D}\colon\mathcal{P}_{S}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{Pr}^{\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{st}})$ be a presheaf of stable presentably symmetric monoidal categories and let $A$ be an object of $\mathcal{D}(S)$ . For $X\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ write $A_{X}=a_{X}^{*}A$ . Assume:

- (1) $\mathcal{D}$ is a Zariski sheaf;

- (2) $\mathcal{D}$ satisfies quasismooth blowup excision, in the sense that for every quasismooth blowup square in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Y}X}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces e}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces b}$ $\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces i}$ we have $A_{X}=i_{*}i^{*}A_{X}\times_{e_{*}e^{*}A_{X}}b_{*}b^{*}A_{X}$ ;

- (3) for every $X\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ and $C\in\mathcal{D}(X)$ , denote $C\langle-1\rangle=\cofib(C\to p_{*}p^{*}C)$ where $p\colon\mathbf{P}^{1}_{X}\to X$ is the projection; then $A_{X}\langle-1\rangle=A_{X}\otimes 1_{X}\langle-1\rangle$ , $1_{X}\langle-1\rangle=a_{X}^{*}1_{S}\langle-1\rangle$ , and $1_{S}\langle-1\rangle$ is invertible in $\mathcal{D}(S)$ .

Also assume the above for $A=1_{S}$ , which actually implies the case for all dualizable $A$ by abstract nonsense. For $X\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ write

$$ F_{A}(X)=(a_{X})_{*}A_{X}. $$

Then the above assumptions guarantee that Construction 4.1 applies to the functor $F_{A}\colon\mathcal{P}_{S}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{D}(S)$ with the $\mathrm{Fin}$ -action given by tensoring with $1_{S}\langle-1\rangle^{\otimes\Phi}$ for $\Phi\in\mathrm{Fin}$ , and hence gives an object $A_{\mathsf{MS}}\in\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{D}(S)$ with $A_{\mathsf{MS}}^{\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty}=A_{\mathsf{MS}}\langle-1\rangle$ .

Moreover, the external tensor product makes $A\mapsto F_{A}$ lax symmetric monoidal under the Day convolution, and thus makes $A\mapsto A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ lax symmetric monoidal wherever it is defined; in particular, $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}$ is a commutative algebra in $\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{D}(S)$ and $A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ is its module.

#### Remark 4.5 (orienting cohomology theories from $4$ -functor formalisms).

Keep the notation and the assumptions of Construction 4.4. By Remark 4.2, in order to linearly orient $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}\in\mathsf{CAlg}(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{D}(S))$ , it suffices to give a section of

$$ F_{1_{S}}(\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}})=\lim_{(X,\mathcal{L})\in{(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}}^{\mathrm{op}}}F_{1_{S}}(X)\xrightarrow{(\mathbf{P}^{1},\mathcal{O}(1))}F_{1_{S}}(\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\tilde{F}_{1_{S}}(\mathbf{P}^{1})=1_{S}\langle-1\rangle, $$

or in other words compatible maps

$$ (c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\colon 1_{S}\langle-1\rangle\to(a_{X})_{*}1_{X})_{(X,\mathcal{L})\in(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}} $$

which, when $(X,\mathcal{L})=(\mathbf{P}^{1},\mathcal{O}(1))$ , is a section of the natural map $(a_{\mathbf{P}^{1}})_{*}1_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}\to\cofib(1_{S}\to(a_{\mathbf{P}^{1}})_{*}1_{\mathbf{P}^{1}})=1_{S}\langle-1\rangle$ . By [4, Remark 3.1.6], such data orient not only $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}$ but also $A_{\mathsf{MS}}$ wherever $A$ satisfies the assumptions of Construction 4.4.

#### Construction 4.6 (Gysin map with coefficients).

Keep the notation and the assumptions of Construction 4.4. For $Y\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , $B\in\mathcal{D}(Y)$ , and $\mathcal{E}$ a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_{Y}$ -module, denote $B\langle-\mathcal{E}\rangle=\fib(p_{*}p^{*}B\to q_{*}q^{*}B)$ where $p\colon\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to Y$ and $q\colon\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E})\to Y$ are the projections. For $A\in\mathcal{D}(S)$ , viewing $A_{\mathsf{MS}}\in\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})\otimes\mathcal{D}(S)$ as a functor $(\mathsf{MS}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathcal{D}(S)$ , Construction 3.1 yields a map

$$ \gys_{A}(i)\colon(a_{Y})_{*}(A_{Y}\langle-\mathcal{N}_{i}\rangle)\to(a_{X})_{*}A_{X} $$

for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . In the special case of $X=S$ , the above map will be $\gys_{A}(i)\colon i_{*}(A_{Y}\langle-\mathcal{N}_{i}\rangle)\to A$ . In the oriented case, as illustrated in the beginning of [3, §6], if $i$ is of codimension $r$ , then $\langle-\mathcal{N}_{i}\rangle=\langle-r\rangle$ is tensoring with $1_{Y}\langle-1\rangle^{\otimes r}$ .

For the rest of this subsection, we take our motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ to be all maps.

#### Example 4.7.

Construction 4.4 applies at least to the following situations:

Fix an integer $n$ invertible in $\mathcal{O}_{S}$ and a $\mathbb{Z}/n$ -algebra $\Lambda$ . Take $\mathcal{D}(X)=\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(X;\mathsf{D}(\Lambda))$ . Then by standard facts on étale cohomology, Construction 4.4 applies for all $A\in\mathcal{D}(S)$ with $1_{S}\langle-1\rangle=1_{S}(-1)[-2]$ the Tate twist.

The Kummer map $\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}\to\mathrm{B}^{2}\mu_{n}$ of étale sheaves gives rise to

$$ c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\in\Gamma(X_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}};\Lambda(1)[2])=\Hom(1_{S}\langle-1\rangle,(a_{X})_{*}1_{X}) $$

functorially for $(X,\mathcal{L})\in(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , and thus a linear orientation of $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}$ .

Construction 4.6 now gives the Gysin map $i_{*}A_{Y}(-r)[-2r]\to A$ for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to S$ of codimension $r$ and every $A\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{\acute{e}t}}(S;\mathsf{D}(\Lambda))$ , recovering Gabber’s étale Gysin map as in [17]. In fact, Theorem 4.9 below will show that our Gysin map generalizes Gabber’s Gysin map to arbitrary quasismooth closed immersions of derived schemes.

Consider the ring stack $\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{a}}/\hat{\mathbf{G}}_{\mathrm{a}}^{\sharp}(-1)$ over $\mathbf{A}^{1}/\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}$ . Via [9, Remark 2.3.8] this transmutes to a stack $X^{\hat{\mathrm{dR}},+}$ over $\mathbf{A}^{1}/\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}$ for any stack $X$ , geometrizing the absolute Hodge-filtered de Rham cohomology, cf. [9, §2.3, §2.5]. Take $\mathcal{D}(X)=\mathsf{D}(X^{\hat{\mathrm{dR}},+})$ . Then by standard facts on de Rham cohomology, Construction 4.4 applies for $A=1_{S}$ with $1_{S}\langle-1\rangle=1_{S}(1)[-2]$ the filtrational and cohomological shift.

The map $\dlog\colon\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}[-1]\to\fil^{1}\mathrm{dR}=[0\to\Omega^{1}\to\Omega^{2}\to\cdots]$ of Zariski sheaves gives rise to

$$ c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\in\Gamma(X_{\mathrm{Zar}};\fil^{1}\mathrm{dR}[2])=\Gamma(X^{\hat{\mathrm{dR}},+};\mathcal{O}(-1)[2])=\Hom(1_{S}\langle-1\rangle,(a_{X})_{*}1_{X}) $$

functorially for $(X,\mathcal{L})\in(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , and thus a linear orientation of $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}$ .

Construction 4.6 now gives the Gysin map $i_{*}A_{Y}(r)[-2r]\to A$ for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to S$ of codimension $r$ and every dualizable $A\in\mathsf{D}(S^{\hat{\mathrm{dR}},+})$ , recovering the Gysin map in [32, 0G82]. In fact, Theorem 4.9 below will show that our Gysin map coincides with the one constructed loc. cit.

Fix a prime $p$ nilpotent in $\mathcal{O}_{S}$ . Take $\mathcal{D}(X)=\mathsf{D}(X^{\mathrm{syn}})$ as in [9, §6]. Then by standard facts on syntomic cohomology, Construction 4.4 applies for $A=1_{S}$ with $1_{S}\langle-1\rangle=1_{S}\{-1\}[-2]$ the Breuil–Kisin twist as in [9, Example 6.1.8].

[7, Proposition 7.5.2] gives rise to

$$ c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{syn}}(X;\mathbb{Z}_{p}(1)[2])=\Gamma(X^{\mathrm{syn}};\mathcal{O}\{1\}[2])=\Hom(1_{S}\langle-1\rangle,(a_{X})_{*}1_{X}) $$

functorially for $(X,\mathcal{L})\in(\mathcal{P}_{S})_{/\mathrm{B}\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{m}}}$ , and thus a linear orientation of $(1_{S})_{\mathsf{MS}}$ .

Construction 4.6 now gives the Gysin map $i_{*}A_{Y}\{-r\}[-2r]\to A$ for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to S$ of codimension $r$ and every dualizable $A\in\mathsf{D}(S^{\mathrm{syn}})$ , recovering the Gysin map in [34, Definition 5.32]. In fact, Theorem 4.9 below will show that our Gysin map coincides with the one constructed loc. cit.

In fact, in the de Rham and syntomic cases, we also expect that Construction 4.4 and hence Construction 4.6 apply for all $A\in\mathcal{D}(S)$ rather than just dualizable ones. This will be a consequence of suitable base change and projection formula, whose proof might involve corresponding solid stacks as in [22].

#### Remark 4.8 (functoriality in cohomology theories).

One advantage of our construction is that it is automatically functorial in maps between cohomology theories, because Constructions 4.1 and 4.4 are clearly functorial. In other words, maps between cohomology theories automatically respect our Gysin map.

### 4.2. Comparison for oriented theories

The following theorem has been invoked several times in the previous subsection; it says that every reasonable construction of Gysin map coincides with ours for oriented theories:

#### Theorem 4.9 (oriented comparison).

Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a stable category and $F$ be an exact functor from $(\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})^{\omega})^{\mathrm{op}}$ to $\mathcal{C}$ . Fix a weak orientation of $F$ , which here means isomorphisms

$$ t_{F}(\mathcal{E})\colon F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)=F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E})) $$

for $Y\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ and $\mathcal{E}$ a locally free sheaf of rank $r$ on $Y$ , for all $r\in\mathbb{N}$ , functorial in $(Y,\mathcal{E})$ along pullbacks, where $\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y$ means $Y\wedge(\mathbf{P}^{1}/\infty)^{\wedge r}$ , which by [3, §3] is also $Y\wedge\mathbf{P}^{r}/\mathbf{P}^{r-1}$ . Assume that there is a map

$$ g_{F}(i)\colon F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)\to F(X) $$

for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ functorially in pullbacks, and that it coincides functorially with $t_{F}(\mathcal{E})$ for zero sections of vector bundles; in other words, functorially for $(Y,\mathcal{E})$ , the map $g_{F}(0_{\mathcal{E}})$ for the zero section $0_{\mathcal{E}}\colon Y=\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{O})\to\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})$ is identified with the composite

$$ F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)\xrightarrow{t_{F}(\mathcal{E})}F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E}))\to F(\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})) $$

where the second arrow is induced by the quotient map $\mathbf{P}_{Y}(\mathcal{E}\oplus\mathcal{O})\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{E})$ .

Then there is a natural isomorphism $g_{F}(i)=F(\gys(i))\circ t_{F}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ .

#### Proof.

For a quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ of codimension $r$ , consider the commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\mathrm{Q}(i))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(X)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces t_{F}(\mathcal{N}_{i})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\gys(i))} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\id} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\id} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces g_{F}(i_{\mathrm{Q}})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces g_{F}(i)} $$

where:

- • the leftmost map $F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)\to F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}))$ is induced by the projection $Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}\to Y$ , and the upper and lower maps $F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}))\to F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)$ are induced by the closed immersions $Y=Y\times 0\to Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ and $Y=Y\times 1\to Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ , respectively; so the two obtuse triangles on the left obviously commute;

- • the sequence $F(X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0)\to F(\mathrm{Q}(i))\to F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ is induced by the fundamental cofiber sequence, and the map $F(\mathrm{Q}(i))\to F(X)$ is induced by the closed immersion $X=X\times 1\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})$ , as in Construction 3.1; so the triangle on the right commutes by the construction of $\gys(i)$ ;

- • $i_{\mathrm{Q}}\colon Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}=\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1})\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})$ denotes the closed immersion of the strict transform of $Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ , so the two parallelograms commute due to the assumptions imposed (or rather the structures assumed) on $g_{F}$ .

The theorem then follows from going through the uppermost and the lowermost routes from the leftmost $F(\Sigma_{\mathbf{P}^{1}}^{r}Y)$ to the lower right $F(X)$ . ∎

#### Remark 4.10 (what is actually used in Theorem 4.9).

Keep the above notation. If there were no orientation for $F$ but only a map

$$ g^{\prime}_{F}(i)\colon F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))\to F(X) $$

functorially for every quasismooth closed immersion $i\colon Y\to X$ in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , then we would still be able to draw the commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Th_{Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\boxtimes\mathcal{O}(1)))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\mathrm{Q}(i))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(X\wedge\mathbf{P}^{1}/0)} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(X)} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces g^{\prime}_{F}(0_{\mathcal{N}_{i}})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F(\gys(i))} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces g^{\prime}_{F}(i_{\mathrm{Q}})} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces g^{\prime}_{F}(i)} $$

where it is not hard to see that $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1})/\mathrm{Bl}_{Y\times 0}(X\times\mathbf{P}^{1})}=\mathcal{N}_{i}\boxtimes\mathcal{O}(1)$ , and the upper and lower maps $F(\Th_{Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\boxtimes\mathcal{O}(1)))\to F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))$ are still induced by the closed immersions $Y=Y\times 0\to Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ and $Y=Y\times 1\to Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}$ , respectively. However, at least a priori, there is no projection map $\Th_{Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\boxtimes\mathcal{O}(1))\to\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i})$ in $\mathbf{P}\mathsf{S}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ for the proof of Theorem 4.9 to work, and this is where the orientation comes in. In other words, the orientation is used only to produce a common section $F(\Th_{Y}(\mathcal{N}_{i}))\to F(\Th_{Y\times\mathbf{P}^{1}}(\mathcal{N}_{i}\boxtimes\mathcal{O}(1)))$ to the above two maps.

#### Remark 4.11.

As illustrated in the beginning of [3, §6], an orientation in the sense of [4, Definition 3.1.2] gives rise to a weak orientation in the sense of Theorem 4.9. As far as I know, weak orientations always occur this way in practice.

## Appendix A Blowup in derived algebraic geometry

In this appendix, we collect definitions and properties of nested blowups of some types of diagrams in derived algebraic geometry used in the main body of the paper, partially generalizing [19]. We start with a simple but useful fact.

#### Proposition A.1 (subtracting a divisor).

Let $X$ be a stack and $D\to X$ be a divisor. Then there is an adjunction

$$ \{\text{closed immersion }Z\to X\}\rightleftarrows\{D\to Y\to X\mid Y\to X\text{ is a closed immersion}\} $$

where the left adjoint is $Z\mapsto Z+D$ (see §1.4(16)) and the right adjoint is $Y\mapsto\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y$ . In this case, we denote $\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y$ by $Y-D$ . Moreover, this adjunction is an equivalence between divisors on both sides.

#### Proof.

Clearly, it suffices to treat the case where $X=\Spec(R)$ and $D=\Spec(R/r)$ . Then a $D\to Y\to X$ where $Y\to X$ is a closed immersion corresponds to a factorization $R\to A\to R/r$ where $R\to A$ is surjective.

We first show that the claimed functor is well-defined, namely that $\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y\to X$ is indeed a closed immersion. Let $I=\fib(R\to A)$ . Recall from [19, Definition 4.3] that $\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y$ , as a functor, takes a ring $S$ to the anima of excessive diagrams

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R/r} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S/J} $$

where $J$ is an invertible ideal of $S$ . Recall that the diagram being excessive means $\fib(A\to R/r)\otimes_{A}S\twoheadrightarrow J$ , or equivalently $rR\otimes_{R}S\twoheadrightarrow J$ , or equivalently $rS=J$ . Therefore, we can rewrite the above anima as that of diagrams

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R/r} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces S/rS} $$

Now this manifestly takes colimits of $A$ as $R$ -algebras to limits of $R$ -stacks, so in order to prove that $\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y\to X$ is a closed immersion, it suffices to treat the case where $A=R/rg$ for some $g\in R$ and $A\to R/r$ is the obvious map, as these generate all surjections from $R$ under colimits. Now this is [33, Lemma 3.4], which says that $\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y=\Spec(R/g)$ .

From the above discussion, it is clear that $Y\mapsto\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y$ is right adjoint to $Z\mapsto Z+D$ . It is also easy to see that $Z\mapsto Z+D$ is conservative. So it remains to see $Y=\mathrm{Bl}_{D}Y+D$ when $Y$ is a divisor, and we can again assume that $Y=\Spec(R/rg)$ , and it becomes the obvious fact that $R/rg=R/g\times_{R/(g,r)}R/r$ . ∎

#### Remark A.2.

In classical algebraic geometry, i.e. if $X$ is a classical scheme and $D\to X$ is a classical divisor locally cut out by a nonzerodivisor, then the adjunction in Proposition A.1 is actually an equivalence. However, this is not true in derived algebraic geometry. In fact, $Z\mapsto Z+D$ does not preserve products as seen in the following simplest example:

- • Let $X=\Spec(R)$ , $D=\Spec(R/r)$ , $Z_{1}=\Spec(R/g_{1})$ and $Z_{2}=\Spec(R/g_{2})$ . Then $Z_{1}\times_{X}Z_{2}+D$ is the spectrum of the ring

$$ R/(g_{1},g_{2})r:=R/(g_{1},g_{2})\times_{R/(g_{1},g_{2},r)}R/r $$

instead of $R/(g_{1}r,g_{2}r)$ whose spectrum is $(Z_{1}+D)\times_{X}(Z_{2}+D)$ . The two rings $R/(g_{1},g_{2})r$ and $R/(g_{1}r,g_{2}r)$ have the same $\pi_{0}$ , but are in general different. For example when $g_{1}=g_{2}=1$ and $r=0$ , they are $R/0=R\oplus R[1]$ and $R/(0,0)=R/0\otimes_{R}R/0=R\oplus R^{\oplus 2}[1]\oplus R[2]$ , respectively.

#### Corollary A.3.

Let $D\to Y\to X$ be closed immersions of stacks where $D\to X$ is a divisor. Then $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X=\mathrm{Bl}_{Y-D}X$ . More precisely,

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Y-D}X+D|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Y-D}X}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Y-D}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

is the universal diagram defining $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ .

#### Proof.

We use the normal deformation studied in [19, §3]. Let

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{N}_{Y}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{D}_{Y}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

be the defining diagram of the normal deformation of $Y\to X$ , and similar for $Y-D\to X$ . Then the defining universal property of the normal deformation gives a map $D|_{\mathfrak{D}_{Y}X}\to\mathfrak{N}_{Y}X$ of divisors in $\mathfrak{D}_{Y}X$ , and using Proposition A.1 one easily sees an isomorphism $\mathfrak{D}_{Y}X=\mathfrak{D}_{Y-D}X$ which takes $\mathfrak{N}_{Y}X$ to $\mathfrak{N}_{Y-D}X+D|_{\mathfrak{D}_{Y-D}X}$ . Now the corollary is reduced to the following easy observation:

- • Let $X^{\prime}$ be a stack over $X$ , $D^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}$ be the base change of $D\to X$ , and $Z^{\prime}\to X^{\prime}$ be a closed immersion. Then a map ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z^{\prime}+D^{\prime}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}$ is excessive if and only if ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z^{\prime}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y-D}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}$ is excessive. ∎

#### Corollary A.4.

Let $Z\to Y\to X$ be closed immersions of stacks. Then

$$ \mathrm{Bl}_{Z}Y=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{Z}X}(Y|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X})=Y|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X}-\mathrm{E}_{Z}X\text{ and }\mathrm{E}_{Z}Y=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{Z}X}(Y|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X}). $$

#### Proof.

Note that $\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}Y$ naturally lives over $\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ , because the square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

is obviously excessive. The corollary then follows from Proposition A.1 by viewing both sides as $\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ -stacks and comparing their defining universal properties. ∎

#### Definition A.5 (strict transform).

In particular, in the setting of Corollary A.4, $\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}Y\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ is a closed immersion, which we call the strict transform of $Y$ in $\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ . Note that the strict transform depends on the map $Z\to Y$ of stacks over $X$ , which is really an extra datum, unlike in classical algebraic geometry.

### A.1. Blowing up a poset of closed immersions

The following definition is central in the symmetric monoidality of the Gysin map.

#### Definition A.6 (blowing up a poset of closed immersions).

Let $P$ be a finite poset. Let $X$ be a stack and $i=(i_{p}\colon Y_{p}\to X)_{p\in P}$ a $P$ -diagram of closed immersions to $X$ . Generalizing Definition 3.3, for a stack $X^{\prime}$ and a $P$ -diagram $i^{\prime}=(i^{\prime}_{p}\colon Y^{\prime}_{p}\to X^{\prime})_{p\in P}$ , a map $f\colon i^{\prime}\to i$ is said to be excessive if for every $p\in P$ ,

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y^{\prime}_{p}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X^{\prime}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y_{p}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f} $$

is excessive, i.e. $f^{*}\mathcal{I}_{Y_{p}/X}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{I}_{Y^{\prime}_{p}/X^{\prime}}$ . The blowup of $i$ , denoted $\mathrm{Bl}(i)$ or $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ , is defined as the final stack along with a $P$ -diagram of divisors, denoted $(\mathrm{E}_{p}(i))_{p\in P}$ or $(\mathrm{E}_{p}X)_{p\in P}$ , and an excessive map of $P$ -diagrams from $(\mathrm{E}_{p}(i)\to\mathrm{Bl}(i))_{p\in P}$ to $i$ . We call $(\mathrm{E}_{p}(i))_{p\in P}$ the exceptional divisors, and more specifically call $\mathrm{E}_{p}(i)$ the exceptional divisor over $Y_{p}$ .

#### Remark A.7.

[26, Definition 3.26] defines a similar notion in classical algebraic geometry, where only the set of closed subschemes matters. Here in derived algebraic geometry, the poset structure really matters. For example, take a closed immersion $Y\to X$ , let $P=\{0,1\}$ , and let $Y_{0}=Y_{1}=Y$ ; if we view $P$ as a discrete poset, the resulting blowup is $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X\times_{X}\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ ; if we instead view $P$ as the usual poset where $0\leq 1$ , the resulting blowup is $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ ; the two are in general different.

#### Remark A.8 (functoriality of blowup).

Definition A.6 has the following functorialities (keeping the notation there):

- (1) Excessive maps of $P$ -diagrams of closed immersions induce maps of blowups.

- (2) For $X^{\prime}\to X$ , let $i^{\prime}$ denote the base change of $i$ to $X^{\prime}$ ; then $\mathrm{Bl}(i^{\prime})=\mathrm{Bl}(i)\times_{X}X^{\prime}$ , and $\mathrm{E}_{p}(i^{\prime})=\mathrm{E}_{p}(i)\times_{X}X^{\prime}$ for all $p\in P$ .

- (3) For $Q\subseteq P$ , there is a canonical map $\mathrm{Bl}(i)\to\mathrm{Bl}(i|_{Q})$ .

#### Lemma A.9.

Let $P$ be a finite poset and $i=(i_{p}\colon Y_{p}\to X)_{p\in P}$ be a $P$ -diagram of closed immersions. Suppose $0\in P$ is an element with $Y_{0}=D$ a divisor in $X$ . Define $i^{\prime}=(i^{\prime}_{p}\colon Y^{\prime}_{p}\to X)_{p\in P\setminus 0}$ as

$$ Y^{\prime}_{p}=\begin{cases}Y_{p}-D,&p>0;\\
Y_{p},&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases} $$

Then $\mathrm{Bl}(i)=\mathrm{Bl}(i^{\prime})$ , where

$$ \mathrm{E}_{p}(i)=\begin{cases}D|_{\mathrm{Bl}(i^{\prime})},&p=0;\\
\mathrm{E}_{p}(i^{\prime})+D|_{\mathrm{Bl}(i^{\prime})},&p>0;\\
\mathrm{E}_{p}(i^{\prime}),&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases} $$

#### Proof.

This is an immediate consequence of Definition A.6 and Corollary A.3. ∎

### A.2. Representability of the poset blowup

In this subsection, we show that in practice the blowup in Definition A.6 is reasonably representable. The main results are Theorem A.20 and Theorem A.22 at the end of this subsection, which say that for a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ as in Definition 2.1 and a base scheme $S$ , the blowup of an excessive diagram in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ stays in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , and blowing up an excessive diagram does not change the total cofiber of the diagram in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ .

Recall that a lattice means a poset that has finite infimums and supremums. For any lattice, we often denote its minimum (empty supremum) by $0$ and maximum (empty infimum) by $1$ .

#### Definition A.10 (excessive diagram).

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and $Y=(Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an $L$ -diagram of stacks. We say that $Y$ is excessive if:

- (1) all maps in the diagram are affine;

- (2) for any downward-closed subset $K\subseteq L$ , the derived $\mathcal{O}_{Y_{1}}$ -algebra

$$ \lim_{k\in K^{\mathrm{op}}}\mathcal{O}_{Y_{k}} $$

is connective, and the structure map $\mathcal{O}_{Y_{1}}\to\lim_{k\in K}\mathcal{O}_{Y_{k}}$ is surjective.

In this case we let $Y_{K}$ denote the spectrum over $Y_{1}$ of the above limit, which is closed immersed to $Y_{1}$ .

If $X$ is a stack, we denote the category of excessive $L$ -diagram of stacks over $X$ by $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Stack}_{X}$ . If $R$ is a ring, we denote the category of excessive $L$ -diagram of $R$ -algebras by $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Ring}_{R}$ , which by definition is the full subcategory of $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Stack}_{R}^{\mathrm{op}}$ consisting of the affines.

There are many equivalent characterizations of excessivity, coming from the following general fact for diagrams in stable categories with t-structures.

#### Proposition A.11.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and $\mathcal{C}$ be a stable category with a t-structure $(\mathcal{C}_{\geq 0},\mathcal{C}_{\leq 0})$ . Let $C=(C_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an $L^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram in $\mathcal{C}$ . The following are equivalent:

- (1) For all downward-closed $K\subseteq L$ , $\fib(C_{1}\to\lim_{k\in K}C_{k})$ is connective.

- (2) For all $\ell\in L$ , $\fib(C_{1}\to\lim_{k\not\geq\ell}C_{k})$ is connective

- (3) For all $\ell\in L$ , $\fib(C_{\ell}\to\lim_{k<\ell}C_{k})$ is connective.

- (4) For all downward-closed $J\subseteq K\subseteq L$ ,

$$ \fib\left(\lim_{k\in K}C_{k}\to\lim_{j\in J}C_{j}\right) $$

is connective.

In particular, for $Y=(Y_{\ell})$ an $L$ -diagram of stacks with affine transition maps, taking $\mathcal{C}=\mathsf{D}(Y_{1})$ and $C_{\ell}=\mathcal{O}_{Y_{\ell}}$ , we get corresponding characterizations of excessivity.

#### Proof.

Equip $L$ with the topology where downward-closed means open, so that it becomes a finite spectral space. View $C$ as a sheaf on $L$ where $C_{\ell}=C(L_{\leq\ell})$ . Then for all downward-closed $J\subseteq K\subseteq L$ , we have $\lim_{k\in K}C_{k}=C(K)$ , and

$$ \fib\left(\lim_{k\in K}C_{k}\to\lim_{j\in J}C_{j}\right)=\Gamma_{K\setminus J}(C) $$

is the local cohomology on the locally closed subset $K\setminus J$ . Recall the following property of local cohomology: for locally closed $U\subseteq V\subseteq L$ where $U$ is open in $V$ , there is naturally a fiber sequence

$$ \Gamma_{V\setminus U}(C)\to\Gamma_{V}(C)\to\Gamma_{U}(C). $$

With these observations in hand, we now start the proof. First, note that $(\ref{excessive-diagram-characterization:two-downward-sets})\implies(\ref{excessive-diagram-characterization:definition})\implies(\ref{excessive-diagram-characterization:generation})$ and $(\ref{excessive-diagram-characterization:two-downward-sets})\implies(\ref{excessive-diagram-characterization:point})$ are trivial.

Let $V=K\setminus J$ . We have noticed that the fiber in (4) is $\Gamma_{V}(C)$ . We do induction on the size of $V$ . The case $V=\varnothing$ is obvious. Otherwise, let $\ell$ be a minimal element in $V$ , so $U=\{\ell\}$ is open in $V$ . Then (3) says that $\Gamma_{U}(C)$ is connective, so (4) follows from induction hypothesis and the fiber sequence displayed above.

We do induction on the size of an upward-closed $Z\subseteq L$ to show:

- •: For all upward-closed $W\subseteq Z$ , $\Gamma_{W}(C)$ is connective, and for all $\ell\in Z$ , $\Gamma_{\{\ell\}}(C)$ is connective.

The case $Z=\varnothing$ is obvious. Otherwise, let $\ell$ be a minimal element in $Z$ . By the induction hypothesis on $Z\setminus\ell$ , it suffices to see that $\Gamma_{\{\ell\}}(C)$ is connective. This follows from (2) and the induction hypothesis, using the fiber sequence displayed above applied on $\{\ell\}\subseteq L_{\geq\ell}\subseteq Z$ , because by upward-closedness of $Z$ we have $L_{\geq\ell}\subseteq Z$ and $L_{\geq\ell}\setminus\ell\subseteq Z\setminus\ell$ . ∎

#### Corollary A.12.

Let $K$ and $L$ be finite lattices, and $Y=(Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an excessive $L$ -diagram. Let $f\colon K\to L$ be a map of posets that preserves nonempty infimums. Then $Y|_{K}$ is excessive.

#### Proof.

It suffices to show that any downward-closed subset $I$ of $K$ is cofinal in the downward closure $J$ of $f(I)$ in $L$ , i.e. that for any $j\in J$ , the anima $|I\cap f^{-1}(J_{\geq j})|$ is contractible. This is clear, as $I\cap f^{-1}(J_{\geq j})$ is nonempty and is closed under nonempty infimums. ∎

#### Corollary A.13.

Let $\Omega$ be a finite set and $X$ be a stack. For each $\omega\in\Omega$ , let $L^{\omega}$ be a finite lattice, and let $Y^{\omega}=(Y^{\omega}_{\ell^{\omega}})_{\ell^{\omega}\in L^{\omega}}$ be an excessive $L^{\omega}$ -diagram with $Y^{\omega}_{1}=X$ . Let $L^{\Omega}=\prod_{\omega\in\Omega}L^{\omega}$ and let $Y^{\Omega}$ be the $L^{\Omega}$ -diagram where $Y^{\Omega}_{\ell}=\prod_{\omega\in\Omega}Y^{\omega}_{\ell^{\omega}}$ for $\ell=(\ell^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}\in L^{\Omega}$ , where the products are taken over $X$ . Then $Y^{\Omega}$ is excessive.

#### Proof.

This is immediate from Proposition A.11 because for $\ell=(\ell^{\omega})_{\omega\in\Omega}\in L^{\Omega}$ ,

$$ \fib\left(\mathcal{O}_{Y_{\ell}}\to\lim_{k<\ell}\mathcal{O}_{Y_{k}}\right)=\bigotimes_{\omega\in\Omega}\fib\left(\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\omega}_{\ell^{\omega}}}\to\lim_{k^{\omega}<\ell^{\omega}}\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\omega}_{k^{\omega}}}\right), $$

where the tensor product is over $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ . ∎

#### Corollary A.14.

Let $X$ be a stack and $L$ be a finite lattice. Then the category $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Stack}_{X}$ has all limits which can be computed pointwise.

#### Proof.

By base change, one immediately reduces the claim to the same claim on the subcategory of those $Y$ with $Y_{1}=X$ . It suffices to treat finite products and sifted limits. The case of finite products follows from Corollaries A.13 and A.12. Pointwise sifted limits in question are computed by pointwise sifted colimits of $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -algebras, which commute with forgetting to $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -modules. Since excessivity is a connectivity condition, it is clearly preserved by $\mathcal{O}_{X}$ -module colimits. ∎

#### Proposition A.15.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and let $R$ be a ring. For $k\in L$ , define the $L^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram $R[x_{k}]$ of $R$ -algebras as

$$ R[x_{k}]_{\ell}=\begin{cases}R[x],&\ell\geq k,\\
R,&\text{otherwise},\end{cases} $$

whose transition map is either the identity or $x\mapsto 0$ . Then $(R[x_{k}])_{k\in L}$ is a family of compact projective generators of $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Ring}_{R}$ .

#### Proof.

For a fixed $k\in L$ , note that:

- • $R[x_{k}]$ is excessive. To see this, take any downward-closed $J\subseteq L$ and we have to see that $R[x]\twoheadrightarrow\lim_{j\in J}R[x_{k}]_{j}$ . If $k\notin J$ it is obvious. If $k\in J$ , let $I=J\setminus J_{\geq k}$ , $J^{\prime}$ be the downward closure of $J_{\geq k}$ , and $I^{\prime}=I\cap J^{\prime}$ . Then

$$ \lim_{j\in J}R[x_{k}]_{j}=\lim_{i\in I}R[x_{k}]_{i}\times_{\lim_{i\in I^{\prime}}R[x_{k}]_{i}}\lim_{j\in J^{\prime}}R[x_{k}]_{j}=R\times_{R}R[x]=R[x], $$

as $J_{\geq k}$ is obviously cofinal in $J^{\prime}$ . This gives the desired surjectivity.

- • For $A\in\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Ring}_{R}$ ,

$$ \Hom(R[x_{k}],A)=\fib\left(A_{1}\to\lim_{\ell\not\geq k}A_{\ell}\right) $$

which is also the fiber in $\mathsf{D}(R)$ by excessivity, and this commutes with sifted colimits of $A$ , so $R[x_{k}]$ is compact projective.

It remains to prove generation. By Corollary A.14 it is easy to see that $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Ring}_{R}$ is presentable, so generation is equivalent to separation, which amounts to saying that if a map $f\colon A\to B$ in $\mathsf{Exc}_{L}\mathsf{Ring}_{R}$ induces equivalences for the above fibers for all $k\in L$ , then $f$ is an equivalence. This is Lemma A.16 below, applied on $\fib(f)$ taken as diagrams in $\mathsf{D}(R)$ . ∎

#### Lemma A.16.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice, $\mathcal{C}$ a stable category, and $C=(C_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ an $L^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram in $\mathcal{C}$ . If for all $k\in L$ ,

$$ \fib\left(C_{1}\to\lim_{\ell\not\geq k}C_{\ell}\right)=0, $$

then $C=0$ .

#### Proof.

This follows from Proposition A.11 by taking “connective” as “zero”. ∎

#### Corollary A.17.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and $Y=(Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an excessive $L$ -diagram of stacks. Let $D\to Y_{0}$ be a map such that the composite $D\to Y_{1}$ is a divisor of $Y_{1}$ . Then $Y-D=(Y_{\ell}-D)_{\ell\in L}$ is also excessive, where we are using the notation of Proposition A.1, viewing everything as closed immersed into $Y_{1}$ .

#### Proof.

Working locally on $Y_{1}$ , we can assume $Y_{1}=\Spec(R)$ and $D=\Spec(R/r)$ . By Corollary A.14, Proposition A.15, and the fact that “ ${}-D$ ” is a right adjoint and preserves limits, it is easy to reduce the claim to diagrams of the form

$$ Y_{\ell}=\Spec\begin{cases}R,&\ell\geq k,\\
R/ar&\text{otherwise},\end{cases} $$

for some $k\in L$ . Now the claim is obvious, as $\Spec(R/ar)-D=\Spec(R/a)$ . ∎

#### Proposition A.18.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and $Y=(Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an excessive $L$ -diagram of stacks. Denote $X=Y_{1}$ . Let $(Q_{m})_{m\in M}$ be a finite family of downward-closed subsets of $L$ , and $Q=\bigcap_{m\in M}Q_{m}$ . Then the map $\bigoplus_{m\in M}\mathcal{I}_{Y_{Q_{m}}/X}\to\mathcal{I}_{Y_{Q}/X}$ is surjective.

#### Proof.

It suffices to treat the cases $M=\varnothing$ and $M=\{1,2\}$ . The former is obvious. For the latter, note that $R=Q_{1}\cup Q_{2}$ is also downward-closed, and $\mathcal{O}_{Y_{R}}=\mathcal{O}_{Y_{Q_{1}}}\times_{\mathcal{O}_{Y_{Q}}}\mathcal{O}_{Y_{Q_{2}}}$ , so $\mathcal{I}_{Y_{R}/X}=\mathcal{I}_{Y_{Q_{1}}/X}\times_{\mathcal{I}_{Y_{Q}/X}}\mathcal{I}_{Y_{Q_{2}}/X}$ is connective. This implies the desired surjectivity. ∎

#### Example A.19.

Let $\Omega$ be a finite set and $(Y_{\omega}\to Y)_{\omega\in\Omega}$ an $\Omega$ -family of closed immersions. For $\Psi\subseteq\Omega$ , let $Y_{\Psi}$ be the fiber product of $(Y_{\omega})_{\omega\in\Psi}$ over $Y$ . We equip the power set $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)$ with the inverse inclusion partial order, denoted $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}$ . Then the $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram $(Y_{\Psi})_{\Psi\subseteq\Omega}$ is excessive by Corollary A.13. Moreover, let $Y\to X$ be a closed immersion and let $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}_{+}=\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}\sqcup\{1\}$ , the lattice obtained from $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}$ by adding an element and setting it as the maximum. Then if we let $Y_{1}=X$ , it is easy to see that the resulting $\mathrm{P}(\Omega)^{\mathrm{op}}_{+}$ -diagram is also excessive.

Fix a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ as in Definition 2.1 and a base scheme $S$ .

#### Theorem A.20.

Let $L$ be a finite lattice and $Y=(Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ be an excessive $L$ -diagram in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ where all the arrows are quasismooth. Denote $\Pi=L\setminus 1$ and $X=Y_{1}$ , and view $Y$ as a diagram $i=(i_{\pi}\colon Y_{\pi}\to X)_{\pi\in\Pi}$ of closed immersions. Then there is a canonical $\Pi$ -family $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\pi}(i))_{\pi\in\Pi}$ of divisors of $\mathrm{Bl}(i)$ such that:

- (1) For all $\pi\in\Pi$ , $\mathrm{E}_{\pi}(i)=\sum_{\rho\leq\pi}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\rho}(i)$ , and for $\pi\leq\pi^{\prime}$ , the map $\mathrm{E}_{\pi}(i)\to\mathrm{E}_{\pi^{\prime}}(i)$ is the obvious one coming from the implication $\rho\leq\pi\implies\rho\leq\pi^{\prime}$ .

- (2) For $\Gamma\subseteq\Pi$ running through all chains in $\Pi$ , i.e. all totally ordered subsets of $\Pi$ , the opens

$$ U_{\Gamma}=\mathrm{Bl}(i)\setminus\bigcup_{\pi\notin\Gamma}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\pi}(i) $$

cover $\mathrm{Bl}(i)$ . In other words, for $\pi,\rho\in\Pi$ incomparable, $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\pi}(i)\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\rho}(i)=\varnothing$ .

- (3) For all $\Psi\subseteq\Pi$ , the fiber product $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i)$ of $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\pi}(i))_{\pi\in\Psi}$ over $\mathrm{Bl}(i)$ lies in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ . In particular, $\mathrm{Bl}(i)=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\varnothing}(i)\in\mathcal{P}_{S}$ .

Under the notation above, we call $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\pi}(i)$ the strict exceptional divisor over $Y_{\pi}$ .

#### Proof.

We do induction on the size of $L$ . When $L=\{1\}$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise, $0\neq 1$ and $0\in\Pi$ . By Definition A.6, $\mathrm{Bl}(i)\to X$ factors through $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}X$ , and in fact $\mathrm{Bl}(i)=\mathrm{Bl}(i|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}X})$ . By Corollaries A.3 and A.4 applied on the divisor $\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}X$ , it is also the blowup of $(\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\pi}\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}X)_{\pi\in\Pi}$ , in which $\mathrm{E}_{0}(i)$ is the blowup of $(\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\pi}\to\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}X)_{\pi\in\Pi}$ . These diagrams stay in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ by Definition 2.1 and stay excessive by Corollary A.17, so we are reduced to the case $Y_{0}=\varnothing$ , where I claim that Zariski locally the theorem reduces to the induction hypothesis:

For $\pi\in L\setminus 0$ , let $U_{\pi}\subseteq X$ be the open complement of the union of $Y_{\rho}$ for all $\rho\not\geq\pi$ . Then over $U_{\pi}$ , the induction hypothesis applies with the smaller lattice $L_{\geq\pi}$ . Now it remains to see that $(U_{\pi})_{\pi\in\Pi\setminus 0}$ cover $X$ , or equivalently:

- • For $M\subseteq\Pi\setminus 0$ with $\inf(M)=0$ , the intersection of $(Y_{m})_{m\in M}$ is empty.

This follows immediately from Proposition A.18 where one takes $Q_{m}=\Pi_{\leq m}$ . ∎

#### Remark A.21.

Note that Definition A.10 does not need the indexing poset to be a lattice. Theorem A.20 might also hold in the same generality, but at this point the author is not sure about this.

#### Theorem A.22.

Keep the notation in Theorem A.20. Consider the $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}=(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i))_{\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ resulting from Theorem A.20(3). Let $\inf\colon\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}\to L$ be the map of posets as the notation suggests. Then there is a natural map $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}\to\inf^{*}Y$ of $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagrams in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , inducing an isomorphism on the total cofibers in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Equivalently, one can form the left Kan extension $\inf_{!}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}$ as an $L$ -diagram in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . Then in fact $(\inf_{!}\mathrm{E}^{\prime})_{\pi}=\mathrm{E}_{\pi}$ , which we view as

$$ \sum_{\rho\leq\pi}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\rho}:=\colim_{\varnothing\neq\Psi\subseteq\Pi_{\leq\pi}}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}\in\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar}}(\mathcal{P}_{S}), $$

and the natural map $\inf_{!}\mathrm{E}^{\prime}\to Y$ induces an isomorphism on the total cofibers in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . In particular, the total cofiber of $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}$ and that of $Y$ in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ are canonically isomorphic.

#### Proof.

We use the same induction process as in Theorem A.20. When $L=\{1\}$ there is still nothing to prove. Otherwise, $0\neq 1$ and $0\in\Pi$ . By Theorem A.20 or rather its proof, the map $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}\to\inf^{*}Y$ of $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagrams factors through the $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagram $F=(F_{\Psi})_{\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ where

$$ F_{\Psi}=\begin{cases}\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\inf(\Psi\setminus 0)},&0\in\Psi;\\
\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\inf(\Psi)},&0\notin\Psi.\end{cases} $$

As in the proof of Theorem A.20, Zariski locally on $F_{\varnothing}=\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}X$ , the induction hypothesis shows that the map $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}\to F$ of $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ -diagrams induces an isomorphism on the total cofibers. Therefore, it remains to show that $F\to\inf^{*}Y$ does too. For this, it suffices to show that for each $\Psi\subseteq\Pi\setminus 0$ , the square

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\inf(\Psi)}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\inf(\Psi)}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y_{\inf(\Psi\cup\{0\})}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y_{\inf(\Psi)}} $$

is a pushout in $\mathsf{Sh}_{\mathrm{Zar},\mathrm{qbu}}(\mathcal{P}_{S})$ . This follows immediately from the definition as $Y_{\inf(\Psi\cup\{0\})}=Y_{0}$ . ∎

#### Corollary A.23.

Keep the notation in the proof of Theorem A.22, and consider the restrictions of the diagrams $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}$ and $F$ from $\mathrm{P}(\Pi)^{\mathrm{op}}$ to $\{\Psi\subseteq\Pi\mid 0\in\Psi\}^{\mathrm{op}}$ . Then the map $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}\to F$ induces an isomorphism on the total cofibers of the restrictions. In particular, the total cofiber of $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i))_{0\in\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ and that of $(\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L}$ are canonically isomorphic.

#### Proof.

By the proof of Theorem A.20:

- • Passing to an open cover of $\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}X$ , one can assume that $L\setminus 0$ has a minimum, and thus is still a lattice.

- • The restricted diagram $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i))_{0\in\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ is obtained by applying Theorem A.20(3) on the $(L\setminus 0)$ -diagram $(\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L\setminus 0}$ .

So the corollary follows from Theorem A.22 applied on $(\mathrm{E}_{Y_{0}}Y_{\ell})_{\ell\in L\setminus 0}$ . ∎

#### Remark A.24.

Similarly to Corollary A.23, one can describe the total cofiber of $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i))_{\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ for any chain $\Psi\subseteq\Pi$ as that of some diagram with final object mapped to $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y_{\pi}}X$ ; in fact, more generally, one can describe the total cofiber of $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Psi}(i))_{\Gamma\subseteq\Psi\subseteq\Pi}$ for any chain $\Gamma\subseteq\Pi$ as that of some diagram with final object mapped to $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{\Gamma}(i|_{\Gamma})$ . We omit the details as this remark is not used in the paper.

### A.3. Pushout-blowups of excessive squares

So far, we have finished developing the theory for symmetric monoidality of the Gysin map. For composition, we need a different blowup construction.

#### Definition A.25 (pushout-blowup).

For an excessive square of stacks

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

as in Definition A.10, its pushout-blowup is the final stack $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}$ along with

- • three divisors $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}$ , $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}$ , and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}$ , and

- • an excessive map from ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z})+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}$ to ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y\sqcup_{W}Z}$ ${\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X}$

We call $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}$ , $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}$ , and $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}$ the strict exceptional divisors over $Y$ , $Z$ , and $W$ , respectively. Here, $\cap$ means taking fiber product over $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}$ , and the reason to use $(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z})+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}$ is that

$$ (\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})\sqcup_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z})+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}}(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})=\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}. $$

#### Remark A.26.

The reader might think that the pushout-blowup in Definition A.25 is the blowup of the poset

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y\sqcup_{W}Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

in the sense of Definition A.6; however, the author does not think so, at least at this point, although this is probably true in classical algebraic geometry, i.e. in the theory of [26, §3.6].

#### Remark A.27.

It is easy to see that the datum of the excessive map in Definition A.25 is equivalent to a map from

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}} $$

to

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

that induces surjections $\mathcal{I}_{Y/X}|_{\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{I}_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}$ , $\mathcal{I}_{Z/X}|_{\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{I}_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}$ , and $\mathcal{I}_{(Y\sqcup_{W}Z)/X}|_{\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{I}_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}$ , or in other words, surjections on the total fibers (of structure sheaves) in the directions $\{Y,W\}$ (upper and lower faces), $\{Z,W\}$ (left and right faces), and $\{Y,Z,W\}$ (the whole cube).

We have to do some preparation before discussing representability of $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}$ .

#### Proposition A.28.

Let

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

be an excessive square. Then the canonical map $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ is a closed immersion, and $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z/\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X}=\fib(\mathcal{N}_{Z/X}|_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z}\to\mathcal{N}_{W/Y}|_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z})(\mathrm{E}_{W}Z)$ . Furthermore, if all four maps above are quasismooth closed immersions, then so is $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ .

#### Proof.

We may assume that all four schemes are affine, so we have ring surjections

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces D} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces B} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A} $$

and we set $I=\fib(A\to B)$ and $J=\fib(C\to D)$ . In this case the excessivity condition is saying that $I\twoheadrightarrow J$ , so the map of the Rees graded rings $I^{\bullet}\to J^{\bullet}$ is surjective, and its $\Proj$ is a closed immersion, showing that $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ is a closed immersion by [19, Theorem 4.7]. To show the equality concerning normal bundles, we use a Yoneda argument. Let $R$ be a ring, $M$ an $R$ -module, and

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces D} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R/K} $$

an $R$ -point of $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z$ , so $J\twoheadrightarrow K$ . Expanding the definitions, we can see that a map $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z/\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X}|_{R}\to M$ amounts to the datum of the following diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces B} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces D} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R\oplus M[1]} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R/K\oplus M/KM[1]} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R/K} $$

where the direct sums denote trivial square-zero extensions. Note that this datum also amounts to a commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{N}_{Z/X}|_{R}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{N}_{W/Y}|_{R/K}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces M} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces M/KM} $$

where the right vertical arrow is $R/K$ -linear and the others are $R$ -linear. Now the claim follows from Lemma A.29 below.

Note that if $\mathcal{N}_{Z/X}$ and $\mathcal{N}_{W/Y}$ are locally free, since $\mathcal{N}_{Z/X}|_{W}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{N}_{W/Y}$ , it is easy to see from what we just proved that $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z/\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X}$ is locally free. Therefore, to justify the last sentence, it remains to see that under its assumption, $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ is a pseudocoherent map. This follows from the fact that $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X\to X$ , $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to Z$ , and $Z\to X$ are all easily seen to be pseudocoherent. ∎

#### Lemma A.29.

Let $R$ be a ring and $K\to R$ be an invertible ideal. Let $N$ be an $R$ -module, $Q$ be an $R/K$ -module, and let $p\colon N\to Q$ be a surjection as $R$ -modules. Then for any $R$ -module $M$ , the datum of a commutative diagram

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces N} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Q} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces M} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces M/KM} \scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces p} $$

where the right vertical arrow is $R/K$ -linear and the others are $R$ -linear, amounts to a map $\fib(p)\to KM$ through taking fibers.

#### Proof.

The lemma is about mapping animas in the category of such $(N,Q,p)$ . Since $(N,Q,p)\mapsto\fib(p)$ preserves colimits, it suffices to prove for a set of generators in the category, which is easy: note that $(R,R/K)$ and $(R,0)$ generate the category, and it is straightforward to verify the claim in these two cases. ∎

#### Remark A.30.

The closed immersion $\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z\to\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ is the derived version of what is classically called the strict transform of $Z$ in $\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ . Note that in the derived setting the strict transform depends on $W$ .

Fix a motivic setup $\mathcal{P}$ as in Definition 2.1 and a base scheme $S$ .

#### Theorem A.31.

For any excessive square in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces W} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Z} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X} $$

where all the arrows are quasismooth, its pushout-blowup $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}$ is representable in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ , and so is the fiber product over $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}$ of every subset of $\{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y},\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z},\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}\}$ . They can be described as follow:

- (1) $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z}\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ .

- (2) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}\mathrm{E}_{Y}X$ .

- (3) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z}\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ .

- (4) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}\mathrm{E}_{Y}X$ .

- (5) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z}\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$

- (6) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}\mathrm{E}_{Y}X$ .

- (7) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Z}\mathrm{Bl}_{Y}X$ .

- (8) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}(\mathrm{E}_{Y}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Z}\mathrm{E}_{Y}X$ .

#### Proof.

We use the equivalent formulation in Remark A.27. Using the universal property of a single blowup, we see that the map there factors through

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{W}Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{W}Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{W}Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} $$

inducing surjections on the total fibers of upper and lower faces, left and right faces, and the whole cube. The surjectivity condition on the whole cube is

$$ (\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y/\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X}\otimes\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{E}_{Z}X/\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X})|_{\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}\otimes\mathcal{I}_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}; $$

by the surjectivity condition on the left and right faces, one can easily see that $\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{E}_{Z}X/\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X}|_{\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}=\mathcal{I}_{(\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}+\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W})/\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}}$ and is invertible, so the surjectivity condition on the whole cube implies the surjectivity condition in the direction $\{Y\}$ , i.e. the left-to-right direction. Therefore, the map further factors through

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} $$

inducing surjections on the total fibers of left-to-right arrows, upper and lower faces, left and right faces, and the whole cube. By the same reason, since the left-to-right arrows are all divisors, the surjectivity condition on the total fibers of upper and lower faces implies the surjectivity condition in the direction $\{W\}$ , i.e. the back-to-front direction. Therefore, the map further factors through

$$ {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X} $$

which is already a diagram of three divisors intersecting in an ambient stack, so:

- (1) $\mathrm{Bl}^{\prime}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ .

- (2) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ .

- (3) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X$ .

- (4) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}=\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X$ .

- (5) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ .

- (6) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{W}Y}\mathrm{Bl}_{Z}X$ .

- (7) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{Bl}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X$ .

- (8) $\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Y}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{Z}\cap\mathrm{E}^{\prime}_{W}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}(\mathrm{E}_{Z}X)_{W}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{E}_{W}Y}\mathrm{E}_{Z}X$ .

That they all remain in $\mathcal{P}_{S}$ follows from Proposition A.28. The other half of the theorem follows by the symmetry of $Y$ and $Z$ . ∎

### A.4. Possible directions of further generalization

#### Remark A.32 (more functoriality).

The blowups introduced in this section should have more functoriality than in excessive maps, just as the $\Proj$ construction of graded rings has more functoriality than in certain graded maps: it is also functorial in certain maps that scale the grading. We plan to pursue such functoriality in future work, as we may need to use it in comparing the $\mathsf{MS}$ of [3] and the $\mathsf{logSH}$ of [10]. It will probably involve the $\Proj$ construction of $\mathbb{N}^{n}$ -graded rings, i.e. an animated version of the theory in [12] and [26].

#### Remark A.33 (pushout-blowup for cubes).

One can define pushout-blowups for excessive $[1]^{n}$ -diagrams similar to Definition A.25, with descriptions similar to Theorem A.31, consisting of $2^{n}-1$ steps of blowups. This will be needed for composing $n$ Gysin maps, generalizing Theorem 3.17 that composes two Gysin maps. Ideally, the author expects a common generalization of such pushout-blowups and poset blowups as in Definition A.6, but he has no concrete idea on this yet.

